#!/usr/bin/env bash
# scripts/bench-campfire — Rails vs the emitted tree, on campfire.
#
# The blog benchmark (scripts/bench) measures a fixture this repo owns;
# the lobsters one (scripts/bench-lobsters) measures a frozen in-process
# visit sequence. This one measures a REAL app over HTTP against REAL
# Rails, both lanes reading the same sqlite file and both signed in as
# the same user — the shape a published claim about "Rails vs
# roundhouse" has to have.
#
# Usage:  scripts/bench-campfire [options] [LANE ...]
#
#   Supported lanes ∈ { rails-lowering | ruby-lowering }
#          (default: rails-lowering ruby-lowering. This harness currently
#           supports only renderer comparisons on a disposable, cache-off
#           Campfire copy; deployed/cache-on and alternate-runtime lanes are
#           not available from this entry point.)
#
#   By default, clone current basecamp/once-campfire main into the result
#   directory and use that immutable source SHA for both Rails and
#   Roundhouse. `--app PATH` selects an explicit pinned checkout instead.
#
#   Historical lane notes below describe the former multi-runtime harness;
#   they are not currently selectable. Only the renderer-only lanes above
#   run from this entry point.
#
#   THE DEPLOYED LANES — what each project would actually run on this box:
#
#     rails          once-campfire AS ITS OWN DOCKERFILE RUNS IT: Thruster
#                    in front of Puma, `(processor_count * 0.666).ceil`
#                    workers x 5 threads from its own config/puma.rb,
#                    jemalloc preloaded, resque-pool alongside, Redis
#                    fragment cache ON, production, YJIT
#     rails-nocache  DIAGNOSTIC, not in the default set: the same shape
#                    with `perform_caching = false`, i.e. Rails actually
#                    RENDERING
#     ruby           roundhouse emit on CRuby, the same Puma shape and
#                    the same allocator
#     rails-lowering / ruby-lowering
#                    one Puma worker, no Thruster/resque-pool, and
#                    fragment caching disabled on both sides. These are
#                    the renderer-focused comparison; Redis remains
#                    configured for Rails' other adapters.
#     spinel         the roundhouse spinel BINARY, workers autodetected
#     jruby          the roundhouse emit for the JVM (`--target jruby`:
#                    the same tree over JDBC), ONE process, one Puma
#                    thread per hardware thread — JRuby has no GVL, so
#                    one process is the whole machine. Heap pinned at
#                    1 GB. (Ladder on the box, 2026-09-21, /rooms/1:
#                    6 threads 1,398 · 12 1,730 · 24 1,586 · 40 1,406
#                    req/s — the "workers x 5 = 40" shape Rails'
#                    formula implies costs it 19%.)
#     truffleruby    the `ruby` tree UNMODIFIED under TruffleRuby (it
#                    compiles the C extensions itself), in the
#                    `ruby-1p` shape: one process x --threads, heap 1 GB,
#                    read against `ruby-1p`.
#
#                    THE DEFAULT SHAPE IS LOCKED, AND THAT IS THE POINT.
#                    TruffleRuby has NO GVL for Ruby code (it has not for
#                    a decade — Benoit Daloze, 2026-09-22). What serializes
#                    its threads here is the GLOBAL C-EXTENSION LOCK it
#                    gives any extension that does not declare
#                    `rb_ext_ractor_safe()` / `rb_ext_thread_safe()`, and
#                    `sqlite3` 2.9.3 declares neither — so every row every
#                    thread steps queues behind every other thread's, and
#                    the ladder INVERTS (4 threads 458 · 8 416 · 12 384 ·
#                    40 293 req/s).
#
#                    `--cexts-lock=false` (TruffleRuby's own documented
#                    benchmarking advice) removes it and is worth 2.5x on
#                    /rooms/1: 432 -> 1,078 req/s at 5 threads, 393 -> 977
#                    at 12, 0 non-2xx, identical tag counts. Single-threaded
#                    it is worth ~3% (2.44 -> 2.36 us/row), which is how it
#                    hides from a microbenchmark.
#
#                    THE LANE RUNS THE DEFAULT anyway, because that is what
#                    `bundle install` gives a deployer, and `BENCH_CEXTS_LOCK=0`
#                    runs the other one. The shape report names which.
#
#   THE OTHER RUNTIME'S STOCK BASELINE:
#
#                    (Stock Rails barely notices the lock — 89 vs 93 req/s,
#                    inside its own run-to-run spread: at ~18 req/s/thread
#                    it never reaches the lock.)
#
#     rails-truffleruby  once-campfire itself under TruffleRuby — the
#                    oracle tree, its own compiled bundle, one Puma
#                    process x --threads (no fork: neither campfire's
#                    worker formula nor resque-pool can run), no
#                    Thruster, 2 GB heap. This is a runtime swap on the
#                    one-process `rails-1p` row, not on `rails`, and the
#                    page says so. No `rails-jruby`: AR-JDBC drives Rails 8.0 only
#                    and campfire tracks rails/rails main.
#
#   WHY THE JVM-FAMILY EMIT LANES MATTER BEYOND THEIR NUMBER. The
#   8-worker `ruby` row is a matched-shape comparison and NOT deployable
#   (the in-process cable registry and job queue do not cross forked
#   workers — see the env notes). One JVM process with forty real
#   threads has ONE registry, ONE fragment cache and ONE queue, so
#   `jruby` is the first multi-core CRuby-family shape on this page that
#   campfire could actually run. TruffleRuby keeps a GIL for Ruby code,
#   so its one process is one core of Ruby execution; that row is a
#   per-core runtime comparison, and its own number says so (measured
#   2026-09-21: the sqlite3 gem's C boundary is 2.6x dearer under
#   Truffle, 2.2 vs 0.85 us/row, and campfire's pages cross it a few
#   hundred times per request — the blog's do not, which is why the
#   emit gains 1.8x on Truffle there and loses here).
#
#   THE ONE-PROCESS LANES — the diagnostic that explains the above:
#
#     rails-1p       Rails, one Puma worker x 5 threads, no proxy, no jobs
#     rails-nocache-1p  DIAGNOSTIC, not in the default set: the same,
#                    rendering rather than reading Redis
#     ruby-1p        the CRuby emit, same
#     spinel-1p      the binary, SPINEL_WORKERS=1
#
# WHY BOTH SETS. The question this page answers is campfire's own — "can
# it beat the current deployment" — and a deployment is a worker count,
# an allocator, a proxy and a job runner, not a renderer. But a single
# deployed number cannot say WHY it landed where it did: Puma's five
# threads share a GVL where the binary's workers do not, so the
# one-process rows are what separate "their runtime is slower" from "we
# gave them fewer cores". Publish the pair.
#
# NO no-JIT LANES. `rails-int` and `ruby-int` measured within a percent of
# their YJIT siblings on these two pages (40 vs 40 req/s, 22 vs 23) and
# cost a seventh of the run to say nothing.
#
# THE DEPLOYED AND RENDERER COMPARISONS ARE DIFFERENT QUESTIONS.
# Current Campfire main has a 64 MiB per-worker full-response cache and
# a separate in-process fragment/record cache. Rails.cache is configured
# with Redis for other facilities; it is NOT the backend for these page
# caches. The deployed `rails` / `ruby` pair retains its cache behavior.
# The `rails-lowering` / `ruby-lowering` pair disables both caches plus
# record snapshots on both sides, uses one worker, and removes Thruster
# and resque-pool from Rails; this is renderer-focused, not a
# compiler-only claim.
#
# Earlier versions exposed `--no-fragment-cache` as a separate diagnostic.
# The current entry point transforms both renderer inputs identically before
# serving them, so there is no separate cache-on/off lane here.
#
# Options:
#   --workers N       Puma workers per lane (default 1). PIN IT: campfire's
#                     config/puma.rb sizes itself to the box otherwise, and
#                     the emit's Puma would not.
#   --threads N       threads per worker (default 5)
#   --duration SEC    measurement window (default 20)
#   --warmup SEC      warmup before each measurement (default 20). The
#                     managed-runtime lanes have FLOORS above it (jruby
#                     60, truffleruby 120, rails-truffleruby 240 — where
#                     each curve went flat on the box for the blog's
#                     same lanes); the record carries the value used.
#   --connections N   wrk concurrency (default 16 — see the note below)
#   --wrk-threads N   wrk client threads (default 2)
#   --runs N          repeated runs per cell (default 3)
#   --endpoint PATH   URL to bench, repeatable. Default: the per-route
#                     suite, the eight routes once-campfire-rust's
#                     bench/run times: room page, messages?before=,
#                     sidebar, searches?q=coffee, the signed-in user's
#                     avatar, a stylesheet, /up, and POST a message.
#                     Prefix POST: for a write.
#   --gzip            send Accept-Encoding: gzip. Off by default: wrk
#                     historically sent no encoding header, so uncompressed
#                     cells stay comparable to older runs. On: CRuby wraps
#                     run_rack in Rack::Deflater and Spinel tep gzips HTML;
#                     CSS/JS stay identity. A gzip run measures what the
#                     lane actually serves.
#   --http-suite      HTTP only: skip cold start and the WebSocket phase.
#                     (The route suite itself is now the default; the POST
#                     goes into a second room so the measured room stays
#                     put. Resolved against the seeded sqlite after
#                     prepare.)
#   --port N          base port (default 4300; lanes get N, N+1, …)
#   --out DIR         results dir (default bench/results/campfire-<stamp>)
#   --users N / --rooms N / --messages N   seed size, passed to
#                     `campfire-oracle prepare` (default 50 / 5 / 100)
#   --no-sockets      skip the WebSocket phase (HTTP + cold start only)
#   --socket-sizes S  socket counts for that phase (default "1000")
#   --socket-cpus C   cores to pin each socket-phase server to (default 0-3,
#                     once.com/campfire's 1,000-user tier)
#   --socket-db PATH  seeded database for the socket phase. Default: the
#                     tier's OWN dataset (1,000 users / 20 rooms), built
#                     once by `campfire-oracle socket-seed` and cached
#                     under build/ — NOT the 50-user HTTP seed, against
#                     which 1,000 sockets become 5 rooms of 200 sharing
#                     50 cookies.
#   --socket-cookies PATH   pre-minted cookie file for it
#   --socket-per-room N     subscribers per room (default 50, which is
#                     the shape the runtime ledger's tiers were measured
#                     at; the sweep derives the room count from it)
#   --skip-prepare    reuse the oracle, transformed source, and emitted tree
#                     already on disk (with matching provenance)
#   --keep            leave servers running on exit
#
# HTTP run: signed-in GET requests against the same transformed source,
# optional gzip, 16 concurrent clients. Only the Rails and emitted Ruby
# renderer lanes are currently supported; no deployed-stack or POST result
# is included in the Lowerer Dividend chart.
#
# WHY 16 CONNECTIONS AND NOT 64. A campfire room page is ~400 KB and
# takes single-digit milliseconds to build; at 64 connections against one
# worker the queue depth, not the app, sets the latency, and both lanes
# converge on the same number. 16 keeps the server the bottleneck.
#
# EVERY REQUEST IS AUTHENTICATED. campfire has no anonymous pages worth
# measuring — an unsigned-in client gets a 302 to /session/new, which is
# a benchmark of a redirect. Each lane signs in through its own real form
# (the two trees do not share a cookie secret) and wrk carries that
# lane's `Cookie:` header.
#
# BOTH LANES READ THE SAME FILE. `prepare` seeds once through Rails and
# copies the result into the emit's storage/, so a row-count difference
# can never explain a throughput difference.
#
# Output:
#   <out>/per-run.json    raw records, one JSON object per line
#   <out>/summary.json    median per (lane, endpoint)
#   <out>/summary.md      rendered table
#   <out>/parity.txt      pre-load response body digest and shape per endpoint
#   <out>/coldstart.json  one record per boot: to an answer, to a page
#   <out>/sockets.json    the WebSocket tier, both lanes, folded from
#                         scripts/campfire-cable-sweep
#   <out>/env.json        provenance: campfire SHA, Rails revision, rubies,
#                         and what each lane ACTUALLY ran
#
# Every one of those is a section on the published page, and
# `.github/workflows/ci.yml` fetches them BY NAME from the bench box — a
# new sidecar that is not added to that list is a section that silently
# does not appear.
#
# Requires: wrk, ruby, bundler, sqlite3, curl, cargo. The jruby /
# truffleruby lanes need those interpreters on PATH and are dropped —
# recorded in skipped.json — when they are not.

set -euo pipefail

REPO_ROOT="$(cd "$(dirname "$0")/.." && pwd)"
. "$REPO_ROOT/scripts/lib/spinel-link-path.sh"   # spinel_link_path: jemalloc's -L on a mac
# A local spinel checkout (AGENTS.md: ~/git/spinel) is enough; CI puts
# the same binaries on PATH. Prepend so `command -v spin` / `spinel` work
# without a profile.
if [[ -x "$HOME/git/spinel/bin/spinel" ]]; then
    PATH="$HOME/git/spinel/bin:$PATH"
fi
# Newest mise install of each tool, if present. Existence-guarded so a
# box without go/elixir is unchanged; not a pinned patch (3.4.11) so
# the next MRI bump does not leave this script looking at a hole.
#
# PLAIN VERSION DIRECTORIES ONLY (`4.0.5`, `1.26.4`). mise installs other
# implementations beside them under the same tool — `truffleruby-40.0.0`
# and `jruby-10.1.1.0` live in installs/ruby — and `sort -V` puts those
# AFTER every MRI version, so "newest ruby" was TruffleRuby. The Rails
# oracle then booted under it and died loading the `debug` gem (unknown
# event: call): every nightly campfire run on the box from 10-05.
mise_prepend_latest() {
    local tool="$1"
    local base="$HOME/.local/share/mise/installs/$tool"
    local d
    [[ -d "$base" ]] || return 0
    d="$(find "$base" -mindepth 2 -maxdepth 2 -type d -name bin ! -path '*/latest/*' 2>/dev/null \
        | grep -E '/[0-9][0-9.]*(-otp-[0-9]+)?/bin$' \
        | sort -V | tail -1 || true)"
    if [[ -n "$d" && -d "$d" ]]; then PATH="$d:$PATH"; fi
    return 0
}
mise_prepend_latest ruby
mise_prepend_latest go
mise_prepend_latest erlang
mise_prepend_latest elixir
export PATH
cd "$REPO_ROOT"

ORIG_ARGS=("$@")

LANES=()
WORKERS=1
THREADS=5
DURATION=20
WARMUP=20
CONNECTIONS=16
WRK_THREADS=2
RUNS=3
ENDPOINTS=()
BASE_PORT=4300
OUT_DIR=""
USERS=50
ROOMS=5
MESSAGES=100
SKIP_PREPARE=0
KEEP=0
SOCKETS=1
SOCKETS_SET=0
SOCKET_SIZES="1000"
SOCKET_CPUS="0-3"
SOCKET_DB=""
SOCKET_COOKIES=""
SOCKET_PER_ROOM=50
APP="$HOME/git/once-campfire"
LATEST_CAMPFIRE=1
LATEST_EXPLICIT=0
APP_OVERRIDE=0
GZIP=0
HTTP_SUITE=0
USER_ENDPOINTS=0

while [[ $# -gt 0 ]]; do
    case "$1" in
        --workers)     WORKERS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --threads)     THREADS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --duration)    DURATION="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --warmup)      WARMUP="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --connections) CONNECTIONS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --wrk-threads) WRK_THREADS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --runs)        RUNS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --endpoint)    ENDPOINTS+=("$2"); USER_ENDPOINTS=1; shift 2 ;;
        --gzip)        GZIP=1; shift ;;
        --http-suite)  HTTP_SUITE=1; shift ;;
        --port)        BASE_PORT="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --out)         OUT_DIR="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --users)       USERS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --rooms)       ROOMS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --messages)    MESSAGES="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --app)         APP="$2"; APP_OVERRIDE=1; shift 2 ;;
        --latest-campfire) LATEST_CAMPFIRE=1; LATEST_EXPLICIT=1; shift ;;
        --sockets)     SOCKETS=1; SOCKETS_SET=1; shift ;;
        --no-sockets)  SOCKETS=0; SOCKETS_SET=1; shift ;;
        --socket-sizes) SOCKET_SIZES="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --socket-cpus)  SOCKET_CPUS="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --socket-db)    SOCKET_DB="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --socket-cookies) SOCKET_COOKIES="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --socket-per-room) SOCKET_PER_ROOM="$2"; shift 2 ;;
        --skip-prepare) SKIP_PREPARE=1; shift ;;
        --keep)        KEEP=1; shift ;;
        -h|--help)     sed -n '2,/^set -euo pipefail$/p' "$0" | sed '$d;s/^# \{0,1\}//'; exit 0 ;;
        rails-lowering|ruby-lowering) LANES+=("$1"); shift ;;
        *) echo "bench-campfire: unknown argument $1" >&2; exit 2 ;;
    esac
done

(( ${#LANES[@]} )) || LANES=(rails-lowering ruby-lowering)
GET_LUA="$REPO_ROOT/scripts/bench-campfire-wrk-get.lua"
POST_LUA="$REPO_ROOT/scripts/bench-campfire-wrk-post.lua"
[[ -n "$OUT_DIR" ]] || OUT_DIR="$REPO_ROOT/bench/results/campfire-$(date +%Y%m%d-%H%M%S)"

ORACLE="$REPO_ROOT/build/campfire-oracle"
EMIT_SOURCE="$OUT_DIR/campfire-emit-source"
EMIT="$REPO_ROOT/build/transpiled-campfire-ruby"
SP_EMIT="$REPO_ROOT/build/transpiled-campfire-spinel"
JRUBY_EMIT="$REPO_ROOT/build/transpiled-campfire-jruby"
# The managed heaps, pinned so the memory column is a working set and
# not the runtime's default share of host RAM (the blog bench's rule).
# 512 MB — the blog's budget — holds campfire's emit but costs it 24% to
# GC pressure (measured 2026-09-21 on JRuby: 4.1k vs 5.4k req/s), so the
# emit gets 1 GB; stock Rails under TruffleRuby gets 2 GB, twice the
# blog's, for a tree that carries twice the working set.
JVM_HEAP="1g"
RAILS_TR_HEAP="2g"
# The binary verifies the session cookie it minted itself, so any stable
# value works — but it has to be the SAME one the cable sweep uses, or a
# pre-minted cookie file cannot be shared between the two harnesses.
SPINEL_SECRET="${SECRET_KEY_BASE:-campfire-oracle-benchmark-secret-not-a-deployment}"
# The three Basecamp ports, built natively on this box. Override the
# roots if the checkouts live somewhere else; missing binaries drop the
# lane rather than aborting the run.
RUST_ROOT="${RUST_ROOT:-$HOME/git/once-campfire-rust}"
GO_ROOT="${GO_ROOT:-$HOME/git/once-campfire-go}"
ELIXIR_ROOT="${ELIXIR_ROOT:-$HOME/git/once-campfire-elixir}"
RUST_BIN="${RUST_BIN:-$RUST_ROOT/target/release/campfire}"
# tobi/campfire-once-ruby-ractor: campfire hand-ported to plain Ruby,
# Falcon on worker Ractors in one process. Bundled in place (vendor/bundle,
# its two C extensions built per its Dockerfile) under this run's Ruby.
TOBI_ROOT="${TOBI_ROOT:-$HOME/git/campfire-once-ruby-ractor}"
GO_BIN="${GO_BIN:-$GO_ROOT/campfire}"
# ABSOLUTE, IMMEDIATELY. Every path derived from here is handed to a
# script that `cd`s — the oracle into its own tree, the cable sweep into
# the server's — and a relative one then resolves against THAT directory.
# The first published run passed `--out bench/results/campfire/<stamp>`
# and lost both deployed Rails lanes and the entire WebSocket phase to
# it, each failing as "No such file or directory" on a log it was trying
# to write. Every validation run before it had used an absolute /tmp
# path, so nothing caught it.
mkdir -p "$OUT_DIR"
OUT_DIR="$(cd "$OUT_DIR" && pwd)"
EMIT_SOURCE="$OUT_DIR/campfire-emit-source"
LOG_DIR="$OUT_DIR/logs"
mkdir -p "$LOG_DIR"

# A lane the harness asked for and could not measure. bench-report draws
# a banner from this file, which is the difference between a page that is
# missing a lane and a page that SAYS it is missing a lane and why — the
# whole point of publishing status rather than results.
SKIPPED="$OUT_DIR/skipped.json"
: > "$SKIPPED"
skip_lane() {   # $1 = lane, $2 = stage, $3 = reason, $4 = log to tail
    local tail_txt=""
    [[ -n "${4:-}" && -f "${4:-}" ]] && tail_txt="$(tail -3 "$4" | tr -d '\r' | tr '\n' ' ' | sed 's/"/\\"/g' | cut -c1-300)"
    printf '{"target":"%s","stage":"%s","reason":"%s","log_tail":"%s"}\n' \
        "$1" "$2" "$3" "$tail_txt" >> "$SKIPPED"
}


# HOW a server died, not just that it did. A lane whose process vanished
# mid-run used to leave three empty wrk outputs, a median computed from
# nothing, and a log holding only the startup banner — the death itself was
# never written down anywhere, so an intermittent one could not be chased.
# The exit status is the only witness a silent crash leaves: 128+N means
# signal N, and SIGSEGV/SIGABRT/SIGKILL are three different bugs.
#
# `kill -0` is not the liveness test here. It succeeds for a zombie — a
# child that has exited and is waiting to be reaped — which is exactly the
# state a just-crashed server is in, so the naive check reports the corpse
# as alive.
server_alive() {
    [[ -n "$SERVER_PID" ]] || return 1
    kill -0 "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || return 1
    [[ "$(ps -p "$SERVER_PID" -o stat= 2>/dev/null)" != *Z* ]]
}

# Returns 0 while the server is up. When it is gone, reaps it, decodes the
# status, and records the death in skipped.json so the published page says
# a lane died and how, rather than showing a hole.
reap_server() {   # $1 = lane, $2 = stage
    server_alive && return 0
    [[ -n "$SERVER_PID" ]] || return 1
    local st=0
    wait "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || st=$?
    SERVER_PID=""
    local how
    if (( st > 128 )); then
        local sig=$(( st - 128 ))
        how="killed by SIG$(kill -l "$sig" 2>/dev/null || echo "$sig") (status $st)"
    else
        how="exited with status $st"
    fi
    fail "$1: server gone during $2 — $how"
    skip_lane "$1" "$2" "server $how" "$LOG_DIR/$1.log"
    return 1
}

step() { printf '\033[36m→\033[0m %s\n' "$*"; }
ok()   { printf '\033[32m✓\033[0m %s\n' "$*"; }
fail() { printf '\033[31m✗\033[0m %s\n' "$*" >&2; }
die()  { fail "$*"; exit 1; }
if (( APP_OVERRIDE )); then
    (( LATEST_EXPLICIT == 0 )) \
        || die "use either --latest-campfire or --app PATH, not both"
    LATEST_CAMPFIRE=0
fi
(( LATEST_CAMPFIRE == 0 || SKIP_PREPARE == 0 )) \
    || die "--latest-campfire cannot be combined with --skip-prepare"
if (( SKIP_PREPARE )); then
    [[ -d "$EMIT_SOURCE" && -d "$ORACLE" && -d "$EMIT" ]] \
        || die "--skip-prepare requires a prior run in the same --out directory"
    [[ -f "$ORACLE/oracle.json" && -f "$EMIT_SOURCE/oracle.json" && -f "$EMIT/benchmark-provenance.json" ]] \
        || die "--skip-prepare requires complete oracle and emitted-tree provenance"
    requested_sha="$(git -C "$APP" rev-parse HEAD 2>/dev/null)" \
        || die "--skip-prepare requires --app to name the original Git checkout"
    ruby -rjson -e '
      oracle_path, source_path, emit_path, requested_sha = ARGV
      oracle, source, emit = [oracle_path, source_path, emit_path].map { |path| JSON.parse(File.read(path)) }
      valid = [oracle, source, emit].all? do |metadata|
        metadata["campfire_sha"] == requested_sha && metadata["benchmark_transform"] == "campfire-lowering-v6"
      end
      exit(valid ? 0 : 1)
    ' "$ORACLE/oracle.json" "$EMIT_SOURCE/oracle.json" "$EMIT/benchmark-provenance.json" "$requested_sha" \
        || die "--skip-prepare provenance does not match the requested Campfire source and transform"
fi
for lane in "${LANES[@]}"; do
    case "$lane" in
        rails-lowering|ruby-lowering) ;;
        *) die "current Campfire harness supports renderer-only lanes; request rails-lowering or ruby-lowering (got $lane)" ;;
    esac
done
[[ -f "$GET_LUA" && -f "$POST_LUA" ]] || die "missing wrk lua scripts next to bench-campfire"

command -v wrk >/dev/null || die "wrk not on PATH"

# For an up-to-date run, take a clean shallow snapshot of canonical
# Campfire main into this run's output directory. The source SHA is then
# captured by campfire-oracle; the Rails lane uses that unmodified source,
# while only the emitted copy receives the compiler-compatibility shim.
# Neither the user's checkout nor its branch is modified.
if (( LATEST_CAMPFIRE )); then
    APP="$OUT_DIR/campfire-source"
    git clone --quiet --depth 1 --branch main --single-branch \
        https://github.com/basecamp/once-campfire.git "$APP" \
        || die "could not fetch latest Campfire from basecamp/once-campfire"
    step "using Campfire upstream main @ $(git -C "$APP" rev-parse --short HEAD)"
fi

SERVER_PID=""
cleanup() {
    local code=$?
    if (( KEEP == 0 )) && [[ -n "$SERVER_PID" ]]; then
        pkill -P "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        kill -TERM "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        sleep 0.3
        kill -KILL "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
    fi
    exit "$code"
}
trap cleanup EXIT INT TERM

# Sum PSS across a PID and its descendants (KB), or nothing where the
# kernel does not offer it (macOS, older Linux).
#
# WHY THIS EXISTS BESIDE THE RSS SUM. Adding up RSS over a process tree
# counts every shared page once per process. A clustered Puma with eight
# workers therefore reads ~3.2 GB where its real footprint is far lower,
# while a single-process binary has nothing to double-count — so the
# error runs entirely in OUR favour, on the biggest memory number the
# page prints. PSS divides each shared page by the number of processes
# sharing it, which is the number a deployer's budget actually sees, and
# it is what the WebSocket phase and the runtime ledger have always used.
sum_pss_kb() {
    local pids="$1" stack="$1"
    [[ -r "/proc/$1/smaps_rollup" ]] || { echo ""; return 0; }
    while [[ -n "$stack" ]]; do
        local next=""
        for p in $stack; do
            for k in $(pgrep -P "$p" 2>/dev/null || true); do
                pids="$pids $k"; next="$next $k"
            done
        done
        stack="$next"
    done
    local total=0 v
    for p in $pids; do
        v="$(awk '/^Pss:/ {s += $2} END {print s+0}' "/proc/$p/smaps_rollup" 2>/dev/null || echo 0)"
        total=$(( total + ${v:-0} ))
    done
    echo "$total"
}

# Sum RSS across a PID and its descendants (KB). Puma's master carries
# almost nothing; the workers are the measurement.
sum_rss_kb() {
    local pids="$1" stack="$1"
    while [[ -n "$stack" ]]; do
        local next=""
        for p in $stack; do
            for k in $(pgrep -P "$p" 2>/dev/null || true); do
                pids="$pids $k"; next="$next $k"
            done
        done
        stack="$next"
    done
    local total=0 r
    for p in $pids; do
        r="$(ps -o rss= -p "$p" 2>/dev/null | tr -d ' ' || echo 0)"
        total=$(( total + ${r:-0} ))
    done
    echo "$total"
}

# Their Dockerfile preloads jemalloc; every Ruby lane here does too, so
# the memory column compares runtimes and not malloc implementations.
# Echoes a path or nothing — a lane that cannot find one records ABSENT
# rather than serving a shape it does not admit to.
find_jemalloc() {
    local c
    for c in /usr/local/lib/libjemalloc.so \
             /usr/lib/x86_64-linux-gnu/libjemalloc.so.2 \
             /usr/lib/aarch64-linux-gnu/libjemalloc.so.2 \
             /usr/lib64/libjemalloc.so.2 \
             /opt/homebrew/lib/libjemalloc.dylib \
             /usr/local/opt/jemalloc/lib/libjemalloc.dylib; do
        [[ -e "$c" ]] && { echo "$c"; return 0; }
    done
    c="$(ldconfig -p 2>/dev/null | awk '/libjemalloc\.so\.2/ {print $NF; exit}')"
    [[ -n "$c" && -e "$c" ]] && { echo "$c"; return 0; }
    return 1
}

# campfire's own worker count for THIS box: `(Concurrent.processor_count
# * 0.666).ceil` out of its config/puma.rb. The emit's Puma has no such
# formula, so the ruby lane is given the number rather than guessing at
# one — the two lanes must be the same shape or the comparison is a core
# count again.
deployed_worker_count() {
    local n
    n="$(getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN 2>/dev/null || sysctl -n hw.ncpu 2>/dev/null || echo 1)"
    ruby -e 'print (ARGV[0].to_i * 0.666).ceil' "$n"
}

# A LANE IS NOT STOPPED UNTIL ITS PORT IS FREE. `stop_lane` waits for the
# process it started, but for the deployed lanes that process is a
# supervisor: `campfire-oracle serve` sends TERM to thruster and
# resque-pool from an EXIT trap and returns immediately, so the listener
# can outlive it by seconds. The next boot then loses the bind and dies —
# and `wait_for_port` cheerfully measures the OLD server, which is how
# three rails-nocache cold starts came back at 27-65 ms against the same
# shape's 4,098 ms elsewhere on the same page.
wait_port_free() {
    local port="$1" budget="${2:-60}" code
    local deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + budget ))
    while (( $(date +%s) < deadline )); do
        code="$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' --max-time 1 "http://127.0.0.1:$port/" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        [[ "$code" == "000" ]] && return 0
        sleep 0.2
    done
    fail "port $port still answers after ${budget}s — refusing to measure a server we did not start"
    return 1
}

wait_for_port() {
    local url="$1" budget="${2:-60}"
    local deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + budget ))
    local code
    while (( $(date +%s) < deadline )); do
        # An answer from the APP means it is up: campfire 302s an
        # unsigned-in client, so `curl --fail` would reject a perfectly
        # healthy boot. But 502 is Thruster answering for a Puma that has
        # not finished booting — accepting it declared the deployed lane
        # ready four seconds early and would have handed the cold-start
        # measurement the proxy's startup instead of Rails'.
        # `|| true`, and it is not decoration. Under `set -e` a failed
        # command substitution exits the harness, and curl exits 7 for
        # "connection refused" — the NORMAL answer while a server is
        # still booting, i.e. every time this loop is doing its job.
        #
        # NOT `|| echo 000`: `-w '%{http_code}'` already prints 000 on a
        # failed connection, so the fallback appended a SECOND one and
        # the guard below compared "000\n000" against "000", found them
        # different, and declared every lane up before it had started.
        code="$(curl -s -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' "$url" 2>/dev/null || true)"
        [[ "$code" != "000" && "$code" != "502" && "$code" != "503" ]] && return 0
        sleep 0.3
    done
    return 1
}

# ── prepare ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

if (( SKIP_PREPARE == 0 )); then
    step "preparing the Rails oracle ($USERS users, $ROOMS rooms, $MESSAGES msgs/room)"
    scripts/campfire-oracle prepare --app "$APP" --out "$ORACLE" \
        --users "$USERS" --rooms "$ROOMS" --messages "$MESSAGES" \
        --renderer-only >/dev/null \
        || die "campfire-oracle prepare failed"

    # FRESH TREES. The emitter writes over its output and never removes a
    # file it no longer generates, so a renamed view outlives the rename:
    # the box's spinel tree carried a 09-25 `index.rbs` beside the current
    # `index_turbo_stream.rbs`, and `spinel --rbs` refused the two
    # declarations of one method (2026-10-07).
    rm -rf "$EMIT_SOURCE" "$EMIT" "$SP_EMIT"
    cp -a "$ORACLE" "$EMIT_SOURCE"
    step "using the same transformed source for the emitted renderer"
    step "transpiling Campfire → $EMIT"
    cargo run --quiet --release --bin roundhouse -- --target ruby "$EMIT_SOURCE" \
        -o "$EMIT" --allow-unsupported > "$LOG_DIR/emit.log" 2>&1 \
        || die "transpile failed (see $LOG_DIR/emit.log)"
    ruby - "$EMIT/boot.rb" <<'RUBY'
path = ARGV.fetch(0)
source = File.read(path)
setting = 'Rails.env_name = ENV["RAILS_ENV"]'
unless source.lines.count { |line| line.chomp == setting } == 1
  abort "expected exactly one Rails.env_name assignment in emitted boot"
end
File.write(path, source.sub(setting, "#{setting}\nRails.cache_enabled = false"))
RUBY
    ruby - "$EMIT/runtime/action_controller/authenticity_token.rb" <<'RUBY'
path = ARGV.fetch(0)
source = File.read(path)
setting = "  set_forgery_flag(true)"
unless source.lines.count { |line| line.chomp == setting } == 1
  abort "expected exactly one forgery-protection enable in emitted token runtime"
end
File.write(path, source.sub(setting, "  set_forgery_flag(false)"))
RUBY
    ruby -rjson - "$ORACLE/oracle.json" "$EMIT/benchmark-provenance.json" <<'RUBY'
source, destination = ARGV
metadata = JSON.parse(File.read(source))
metadata = metadata.slice("campfire_sha", "benchmark_transform")
metadata["benchmark_cache_override"] = "disabled in disposable emitted boot; does not affect generated applications"
metadata["benchmark_forgery_override"] = "disabled in disposable emitted token runtime; Campfire header_only strategy is not modeled"
File.write(destination, JSON.pretty_generate(metadata) + "\n")
RUBY

    # Use the same Puma release as the Rails source lockfile so the matched
    # renderer pair does not accidentally compare app-server versions.
    rails_puma="$(awk '/^    puma \(/ {gsub(/[()]/, "", $2); print $2; exit}' "$ORACLE/Gemfile.lock")"
    [[ -n "$rails_puma" ]] || die "could not read Puma version from Campfire Gemfile.lock"
    ruby - "$EMIT/Gemfile" "$rails_puma" <<'RUBY'
path, version = ARGV
source = File.read(path)
unless source.lines.count { |line| line.chomp == 'gem "puma"' } == 1
  abort "expected exactly one unpinned Puma dependency in emitted Gemfile"
end
File.write(path, source.sub('gem "puma"', %(gem "puma", "=#{version}")))
RUBY

    step "bundling the emit"
    (cd "$EMIT" && bundle install >/dev/null 2>&1) || die "emit bundle install failed"

    step "building the emit's assets"
    (cd "$EMIT" && rake assets >/dev/null 2>&1) || step "  (no assets task — continuing)"
fi

# ── the spinel binary ────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Campfire compiles, builds and serves on spinel now. Until 2026-09-04
# this harness emitted the tree for the ARCHIVE only, with a comment
# saying it "does not compile yet" — true when written, and the reason
# the published page had no binary lane while the binary was holding five
# thousand WebSocket connections in another harness.
#
# Strict emit (no --allow-unsupported), because CI holds campfire's
# strict emit at zero errors and a regression should be loud here.
build_spinel_binary() {
    step "transpiling campfire → spinel (strict)"
    cargo run --quiet --release --bin roundhouse -- --target spinel "$APP" \
        -o "$SP_EMIT" > "$LOG_DIR/emit-spinel.log" 2>&1 \
        || { fail "strict spinel emit failed (see $LOG_DIR/emit-spinel.log)"; return 1; }
    command -v spin >/dev/null 2>&1 \
        || { fail "spin not on PATH — cannot build the spinel binary"; return 1; }
    spinel_link_path
    step "building the spinel binary (a few minutes)"
    ( cd "$SP_EMIT" && make build ) > "$LOG_DIR/build-spinel.log" 2>&1 \
        || { fail "spinel build failed (see $LOG_DIR/build-spinel.log)"; return 1; }
    [[ -x "$SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog" ]] \
        || { fail "spinel build produced no binary"; return 1; }
    # The binary serves static/assets, which only `make assets` fills:
    # without it every stylesheet the room page links is a 404.
    step "staging the spinel tree's assets"
    ( cd "$SP_EMIT" && make assets ) > "$LOG_DIR/assets-spinel.log" 2>&1 \
        || fail "spinel make assets failed (see $LOG_DIR/assets-spinel.log)"
    ok "spinel binary at $SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog"
}

WANT_SPINEL=0
for _l in "${LANES[@]}"; do [[ "$_l" == spinel* ]] && WANT_SPINEL=1; done
# `--skip-prepare` keeps the binary too, when there is one: the flag's
# promise is "reuse what is on disk", and an A/B that rebuilds the emit
# with a different `spin.toml` (an allocator, say) has to be able to run
# the harness against THAT binary rather than a fresh emit's.
if (( WANT_SPINEL )) && { (( SKIP_PREPARE == 0 )) || [[ ! -x "$SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog" ]]; }; then
    if ! build_spinel_binary; then
        fail "dropping the spinel lanes"
        NEW_LANES=()
        for _l in "${LANES[@]}"; do
            if [[ "$_l" == spinel* ]]; then
                skip_lane "$_l" "build" "the spinel binary could not be built" "$LOG_DIR/build-spinel.log"
            else
                NEW_LANES+=("$_l")
            fi
        done
        LANES=("${NEW_LANES[@]}")
    fi
fi

# ── the JVM-family lanes ─────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# Built the way scripts/bench builds the blog's: the jruby tree is its
# own emit (`--target jruby`, the same tree over JDBC) with a fresh
# JVM-only lock; the truffleruby lane is the `ruby` tree UNMODIFIED with
# a bundle TruffleRuby compiled into its own gem home; the stock Rails
# lane is the oracle with a second bundle beside the CRuby one. A lane
# whose interpreter is not on PATH is dropped and recorded, not
# silently skipped.
drop_lane() {   # $1 = lane, $2 = stage, $3 = reason, $4 = log
    skip_lane "$1" "$2" "$3" "${4:-}"
    local keep=()
    for _l in "${LANES[@]}"; do [[ "$_l" == "$1" ]] || keep+=("$_l"); done
    LANES=("${keep[@]}")
}
wants_lane() { local _l; for _l in "${LANES[@]}"; do [[ "$_l" == "$1" ]] && return 0; done; return 1; }

# One Puma thread per hardware thread for the JVM lane: no GVL, no fork,
# so one process is the whole box and threads past the cores only queue
# (the ladder is in the header). The TruffleRuby lanes take --threads,
# the one-process shape of the rows they are read against.
jvm_threads() { getconf _NPROCESSORS_ONLN 2>/dev/null || sysctl -n hw.ncpu 2>/dev/null || echo 1; }

if wants_lane jruby; then
    if ! command -v jruby >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        fail "jruby not on PATH — dropping the jruby lane"
        drop_lane jruby build "jruby is not on PATH"
    elif (( SKIP_PREPARE == 0 )) || [[ ! -d "$JRUBY_EMIT" ]]; then
        step "transpiling campfire → $JRUBY_EMIT (jruby)"
        if ! cargo run --quiet --release --bin roundhouse -- --target jruby "$APP" \
                -o "$JRUBY_EMIT" --allow-unsupported > "$LOG_DIR/emit-jruby.log" 2>&1; then
            fail "jruby emit failed (see $LOG_DIR/emit-jruby.log) — dropping the lane"
            drop_lane jruby build "the jruby emit failed" "$LOG_DIR/emit-jruby.log"
        else
            # The emit drops the MRI lock so this resolves a JVM-only one;
            # a lock left by an earlier run survives the re-emit and can
            # carry a CRuby platform, so re-resolve whenever the bundle is
            # not satisfied, clearing any stale lock first.
            step "bundling the jruby emit (resolves a JVM-only lock)"
            if ! (cd "$JRUBY_EMIT" && jruby -S bundle check >/dev/null 2>&1); then
                rm -f "$JRUBY_EMIT/Gemfile.lock"
                (cd "$JRUBY_EMIT" && jruby -S bundle install >"$LOG_DIR/bundle-jruby.log" 2>&1) || {
                    fail "jruby bundle install failed (see $LOG_DIR/bundle-jruby.log) — dropping the lane"
                    drop_lane jruby build "jruby bundle install failed" "$LOG_DIR/bundle-jruby.log"
                }
            fi
        fi
    fi
fi

if wants_lane truffleruby; then
    if ! command -v truffleruby >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        fail "truffleruby not on PATH — dropping the truffleruby lane"
        drop_lane truffleruby build "truffleruby is not on PATH"
    elif [[ ! -d "$EMIT" ]]; then
        fail "no CRuby emit at $EMIT for the truffleruby lane — dropping it"
        drop_lane truffleruby build "no CRuby emit to run under truffleruby"
    else
        # Against the EMITTED tree's Gemfile, into TruffleRuby's own gem
        # home: the tree is re-emitted every run, so nothing installed
        # inside it would survive.
        step "bundling the ruby emit under truffleruby (compiles the C extensions once)"
        if ! (cd "$EMIT" && truffleruby -S bundle check >/dev/null 2>&1); then
            (cd "$EMIT" && truffleruby -S bundle install >"$LOG_DIR/bundle-truffleruby.log" 2>&1) || {
                fail "truffleruby bundle install failed (see $LOG_DIR/bundle-truffleruby.log) — dropping the lane"
                drop_lane truffleruby build "truffleruby bundle install failed" "$LOG_DIR/bundle-truffleruby.log"
            }
        fi
    fi
fi

if wants_lane rust && [[ ! -x "$RUST_BIN" ]]; then
    fail "no rust campfire binary at $RUST_BIN — dropping the rust lane"
    drop_lane rust build "once-campfire-rust binary not built (cargo build --release -p campfire)"
fi
if wants_lane tobi && ! ( cd "$TOBI_ROOT" 2>/dev/null && bundle check >/dev/null 2>&1 &&
                          [[ -f lib/campfire_vips.so && -f vendor/gems/extralite/lib/extralite_ext.so ]] ); then
    fail "$TOBI_ROOT is not bundled and built — dropping the tobi lane"
    drop_lane tobi build "campfire-once-ruby-ractor not ready (bundle install + the extralite and campfire_vips extensions)"
fi
if wants_lane go && [[ ! -x "$GO_BIN" ]]; then
    fail "no go campfire binary at $GO_BIN — dropping the go lane"
    drop_lane go build "once-campfire-go binary not built (bin/build)"
fi
if wants_lane elixir; then
    if ! command -v mix >/dev/null 2>&1 || [[ ! -d "$ELIXIR_ROOT" ]]; then
        fail "mix or $ELIXIR_ROOT missing — dropping the elixir lane"
        drop_lane elixir build "once-campfire-elixir is not ready (mix + checkout)"
    fi
fi

if wants_lane rails-truffleruby; then
    if ! command -v truffleruby >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        fail "truffleruby not on PATH — dropping the rails-truffleruby lane"
        drop_lane rails-truffleruby build "truffleruby is not on PATH"
    else
        step "bundling the Rails oracle under truffleruby"
        scripts/campfire-oracle bundle --runtime truffleruby --out "$ORACLE" \
            > "$LOG_DIR/bundle-rails-truffleruby.log" 2>&1 || {
            fail "the oracle's truffleruby bundle failed (see $LOG_DIR/bundle-rails-truffleruby.log) — dropping the lane"
            drop_lane rails-truffleruby build "the oracle's truffleruby bundle failed" "$LOG_DIR/bundle-rails-truffleruby.log"
        }
    fi
fi

# Five signed-in HTTP workloads, resolved against THIS seed. Writes go
# to a second room so the measured room page stays the same size across
# POST cells.
SEED_DB="$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3"
[[ -f "$SEED_DB" ]] || die "no seeded production DB at $ORACLE — run without --skip-prepare"
resolve_http_suite() {
    local db="$1"
    [[ -f "$db" ]] || die "no seed database at $db"
    python3 - "$db" <<'PY'
import sqlite3, sys
db = sqlite3.connect(sys.argv[1])
rooms = [r[0] for r in db.execute("select id from rooms order by id")]
if not rooms:
    sys.exit("no rooms in seed")
room = rooms[0]
write_room = rooms[1] if len(rooms) > 1 else rooms[0]
n = db.execute("select count(*) from messages where room_id=?", (room,)).fetchone()[0]
# Mid-history page so paging is not the newest 40.
off = max(n // 2, 1)
row = db.execute(
    "select id from messages where room_id=? order by created_at, id limit 1 offset ?",
    (room, off - 1),
).fetchone()
if not row:
    row = db.execute(
        "select id from messages where room_id=? order by id", (room,)
    ).fetchone()
before_id = row[0] if row else 1
print(f"/rooms/{room}")
print(f"/rooms/{room}/messages?before={before_id}")
print("/users/me/sidebar")
print("/searches?q=coffee")
# Per lane, scraped from its own room page: the signed avatar token and
# the stylesheet digest differ between trees (see ep_path).
print("/users/{avatar}/avatar")
print("{css}")
print("/up")
print(f"POST:/rooms/{write_room}/messages")
PY
}

if (( USER_ENDPOINTS == 0 )); then
    local_routes="$(resolve_http_suite "$SEED_DB")" || die "could not resolve HTTP suite routes from $SEED_DB"
    mapfile -t ENDPOINTS <<< "$local_routes"
    (( ${#ENDPOINTS[@]} >= 8 )) || die "HTTP suite resolution returned ${#ENDPOINTS[@]} routes: ${ENDPOINTS[*]}"
fi
if (( HTTP_SUITE )); then
    # HTTP only. Cable stays behind --sockets.
    if (( SOCKETS_SET == 0 )); then
        SOCKETS=0
    fi
    step "HTTP suite routes: ${ENDPOINTS[*]}"
fi

ep_is_post() { [[ "$1" == POST:* ]]; }
# `{avatar}` and `{css}` are the two routes whose path is the lane's own:
# an avatar token is signed with the lane's secret, a stylesheet carries
# its tree's digest. Both come from the lane's room page (BENCH_AVATAR,
# BENCH_CSS, set after sign-in); the record keeps the placeholder so the
# cell lines up across lanes.
ep_path() {
    local ep="$1"
    if ep_is_post "$ep"; then ep="${ep#POST:}"; fi
    ep="${ep//\{avatar\}/${BENCH_AVATAR:-}}"
    ep="${ep//\{css\}/${BENCH_CSS:-}}"
    echo "$ep"
}
ep_parity_name() { echo "$1" | tr '/:?=&{}' '_______'; }

# Copy the oracle seed into a destination tree. WAL/SHM go with it: a
# stale -wal beside a freshly copied database is a different database.
stage_sqlite() {
    local dest="$1"
    mkdir -p "$(dirname "$dest")"
    cp "$SEED_DB" "$dest"
    rm -f "${dest}-wal" "${dest}-shm"
}

# THE SAME ROWS, ALWAYS. Copied on every run, not only on prepare: a
# previous run's POSTs would otherwise leave the two databases apart, and
# a benchmark whose lanes read different data is not one.
# Native once-campfire-* trees are overwritten only when that lane runs.
stage_seeded_db() {
    [[ -f "$SEED_DB" ]] || die "no seeded production DB at $ORACLE"
    stage_sqlite "$EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3"
    if [[ -d "$SP_EMIT" ]]; then stage_sqlite "$SP_EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3"; fi
    if [[ -d "$JRUBY_EMIT" ]]; then stage_sqlite "$JRUBY_EMIT/storage/development.sqlite3"; fi
    if wants_lane rust && [[ -d "$RUST_ROOT" ]]; then
        stage_sqlite "$RUST_ROOT/storage/db/production.sqlite3"
    fi
    if wants_lane go && [[ -d "$GO_ROOT" ]]; then
        stage_sqlite "$GO_ROOT/storage/db/production.sqlite3"
    fi
    if wants_lane tobi && [[ -d "$TOBI_ROOT" ]]; then
        stage_sqlite "$TOBI_ROOT/storage/db/production.sqlite3"
    fi
    if wants_lane elixir && [[ -d "$ELIXIR_ROOT" ]]; then
        stage_sqlite "$ELIXIR_ROOT/var/production.sqlite3"
    fi
    # Rails oracle serves production.sqlite3 in place. Snapshot it so a
    # POST lane can be rolled back before the next one boots.
    cp "$SEED_DB" "$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3.clean"
    rm -f "$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3-wal" "$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3-shm"
}

restore_seeded_db() {
    local clean="$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3.clean"
    [[ -f "$clean" ]] || return 0
    cp "$clean" "$SEED_DB"
    rm -f "$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3-wal" "$ORACLE/storage/db/production.sqlite3-shm"
    stage_seeded_db
}

csrf_token_from() {
    local html="$1"
    python3 - "$html" <<'PY'
import re, sys
html = open(sys.argv[1], encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
m = re.search(r'name="csrf-token" content="([^"]*)"', html) \
    or re.search(r'name="authenticity_token" value="([^"]*)"', html)
print(m.group(1) if m else "")
PY
}

# The lane-specific half of the per-route suite, read off the lane's own
# room page: the first avatar image's token and the first stylesheet.
scrape_from() {
    python3 - "$1" "$2" <<'PY'
import re, sys
html = open(sys.argv[1], encoding="utf-8", errors="replace").read()
if sys.argv[2] == "avatar":
    m = re.search(r'/users/([^/"?]+)/avatar', html)
else:
    m = re.search(r'<link[^>]*rel="stylesheet"[^>]*href="([^"]+\.css)"', html) \
        or re.search(r'<link[^>]*href="([^"]+\.css)"[^>]*rel="stylesheet"', html)
print(m.group(1) if m else "")
PY
}

step "staging the seeded DB into the emit"
stage_seeded_db

# ── per-lane lifecycle ───────────────────────────────────────────────

start_lane() {
    local lane="$1" port="$2"
    # OVERRIDABLE for the same reason `shape` below is, and it took the
    # first nightly with GC capture to notice: `cold_start` boots each
    # lane three MORE times after the measured runs, and every boot
    # reopened this path with `>`. So the lane log a reader (or a
    # parser) sees was the last cold-start boot -- one page served, one
    # collection -- and the measured run's output had been truncated
    # away hours earlier. Nothing noticed while these logs held only a
    # banner; `gc.json` came out saying "1 collections, 0.0s" for a lane
    # that had just served thousands of requests.
    local log="${LOG_OVERRIDE:-$LOG_DIR/$lane.log}"
    # The shape record belongs to the MEASURED boot. `cold_start` boots
    # each lane three more times and would otherwise leave the file
    # holding whatever its last boot managed to write — which is how
    # rails-nocache's row on the page shrank to one line.
    local shape="${SHAPE_OVERRIDE:-$LOG_DIR/shape-$lane.txt}"
    case "$lane" in
        rails|rails-nocache)
            # AS THEIR DOCKERFILE RUNS IT. `--deployed` buys campfire's
            # own worker formula instead of a pinned count, Thruster,
            # and resque-pool. NOT jemalloc, which this comment used to
            # claim: `campfire-oracle` preloads it on EVERY ruby lane,
            # deployed or diagnostic ("jemalloc on EVERY Ruby lane" at
            # its serve path), and the shape reports confirm it —
            # `shape-rails-1p.txt` names the same libjemalloc as
            # `shape-rails.txt`. The binary links it at build time
            # (`--link -ljemalloc`), so the ALLOCATOR IS CONSTANT across
            # all six lanes and the memory column compares runtimes
            # rather than mallocs.
            local nocache=""; [[ "$lane" == "rails-nocache" ]] && nocache="--no-fragment-cache"
            scripts/campfire-oracle serve --deployed --out "$ORACLE" --port "$port" \
                --threads "$THREADS" --shape-report "$shape" \
                $nocache > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        rails-lowering)
            # Renderer comparison: one worker, no Thruster/resque-pool,
            # and Rails' null store, matching ruby-lowering below.
            scripts/campfire-oracle serve --out "$ORACLE" --port "$port" \
                --workers 1 --threads "$THREADS" --shape-report "$shape" \
                --no-fragment-cache > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        rails-1p|rails-nocache-1p)
            local nc1=""; [[ "$lane" == "rails-nocache-1p" ]] && nc1="--no-fragment-cache"
            scripts/campfire-oracle serve --out "$ORACLE" --port "$port" \
                --workers 1 --threads "$THREADS" --shape-report "$shape" \
                $nc1 > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        ruby|ruby-1p)
            # RAILS_ENV=production, for the same reason the oracle serves it
            # (see campfire-oracle's header: development measured 11.4 req/s
            # against production's 141.1 — the SAME app, 12x apart). The emit
            # reads `Rails.env` too, and with it unset every ruby lane booted
            # "development" while the lane it is compared against booted
            # production. On campfire's benchmarked routes that costs nothing
            # — the only env branch in the emitted tree is in
            # `users/profiles/show` — but an asymmetry that happens to be
            # free today is not one to leave in a published comparison. The
            # database does not move with it: the emit resolves its path from
            # DB_PATH or the literal `storage/development.sqlite3`.
            #
            # THE SAME ALLOCATOR AS THEIRS. Their Dockerfile preloads
            # jemalloc; this lane is Ruby too, and a memory column that
            # compared their allocator against ours would be measuring
            # malloc rather than runtimes.
            local jem; jem="$(find_jemalloc || true)"
            local w=1
            [[ "$lane" == "ruby" ]] && w="$(deployed_worker_count)"
            echo "jemalloc=${jem:-ABSENT}" > "$shape"
            echo "workers=$w" >> "$shape"
            ( cd "$EMIT" && exec env RAILS_ENV=production RUBY_YJIT_ENABLE=1 \
                RAILS_MAX_THREADS="$THREADS" ${jem:+LD_PRELOAD="$jem"} \
                bundle exec puma -p "$port" -w "$w" -t "$THREADS:$THREADS" \
                config.ru ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        ruby-lowering)
            # Same Ruby/Puma/worker shape as rails-lowering. Disable the
            # Rails-level cache switch, custom response-cache budget, and
            # runtime store so no cache path is exercised in this lane.
            local jem; jem="$(find_jemalloc || true)"
            echo "jemalloc=${jem:-ABSENT}" > "$shape"
            echo "workers=1" >> "$shape"
            echo "fragment-and-response-cache=disabled" >> "$shape"
            echo "response-cache-budget-mb=0" >> "$shape"
            echo "forgery-protection=disabled in disposable emitted token runtime (header_only strategy unsupported)" >> "$shape"
            ( cd "$EMIT" && exec env RAILS_ENV=production SECRET_KEY_BASE="$SPINEL_SECRET" \
                DISABLE_FRAGMENT_CACHE=1 \
                CAMPFIRE_RESPONSE_CACHE_MB=0 \
                RUBY_YJIT_ENABLE=1 RAILS_MAX_THREADS="$THREADS" \
                ${jem:+LD_PRELOAD="$jem"} \
                bundle exec puma -p "$port" -w 1 -t "$THREADS:$THREADS" \
                config.ru ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        jruby)
            # One process, a thread per hardware thread, no GVL: the JVM
            # runs Ruby threads in parallel, so the shape Rails reaches
            # with eight forked workers is one process here — and unlike
            # the `ruby` row, the in-process cable registry and job queue
            # are shared by every thread, so this shape is one campfire
            # could deploy.
            # Heap pinned (Xms=Xmx) so RSS is a working set, not lazy
            # growth. No jemalloc: the JVM does not malloc its heap.
            local jt; jt="$(jvm_threads)"
            printf 'runtime=jruby\nheap=%s\nworkers=0 (one process, no fork)\nthreads=%s\n' \
                "$JVM_HEAP" "$jt" > "$shape"
            ( cd "$JRUBY_EMIT" && exec env RAILS_ENV=production \
                RAILS_MAX_THREADS="$jt" WEB_CONCURRENCY=0 PORT="$port" \
                JRUBY_OPTS="-J-Xmx$JVM_HEAP -J-Xms$JVM_HEAP" \
                jruby -S bundle exec puma -C config/puma.rb config.ru ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        truffleruby)
            # The `ruby` tree, unmodified, under TruffleRuby: same emit,
            # same Puma, same database, the `ruby-1p` shape (one process
            # x --threads). TruffleRuby keeps a GIL for Ruby code, so this
            # is one core of Ruby execution plus the compiler and GC
            # threads, and more threads only contend (header ladder).
            local tt="$THREADS"
            # See the header: the C-extension lock, not a GVL, is what
            # serializes this lane. Default = locked (what a deployer
            # gets); BENCH_CEXTS_LOCK=0 measures the other shape. Either
            # way the shape report says which one ran.
            local tropt="--vm.Xmx$JVM_HEAP" cexts=1
            if [[ "${BENCH_CEXTS_LOCK:-1}" == "0" ]]; then
                tropt="$tropt --experimental-options --cexts-lock=false"; cexts=0
            fi
            printf 'runtime=truffleruby\nheap=%s\nworkers=0 (one process, no fork)\nthreads=%s\ncexts-lock=%s\n' \
                "$JVM_HEAP" "$tt" "$cexts" > "$shape"
            ( cd "$EMIT" && exec env RAILS_ENV=production \
                RAILS_MAX_THREADS="$tt" WEB_CONCURRENCY=0 PORT="$port" \
                TRUFFLERUBYOPT="$tropt" \
                truffleruby -S bundle exec puma -C config/puma.rb config.ru ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        rails-truffleruby)
            # Stock campfire under TruffleRuby — the oracle, its own
            # compiled bundle, one Puma process. See campfire-oracle's
            # `cmd_serve_truffleruby` for what cannot be the same.
            scripts/campfire-oracle serve --runtime truffleruby --heap "$RAILS_TR_HEAP" \
                --out "$ORACLE" --port "$port" --threads "$THREADS" \
                --shape-report "$shape" > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        spinel|spinel-1p)
            # THE BINARY. One process either way — it has no cluster mode
            # — so the lane pair is its OS worker count: autodetected for
            # the deployed row, one for the diagnostic. No allocator knob:
            # it does not use Ruby's.
            local workers_env=()
            if [[ "$lane" == "spinel-1p" ]]; then
                workers_env=(SPINEL_WORKERS=1); echo "workers=1" > "$shape"
            else
                echo "workers=autodetected" > "$shape"
            fi
            # THE COLLECTOR IS THIS LANE'S CEILING, so measuring
            # everything except it was the gap: these logs carried two
            # lines and no GC data at all. `SPINEL_GC_STATS` gives the
            # collection count and the stopped share; `SPINEL_GC_PHASES`
            # (matz/spinel#4382) splits that into mark / old sweep /
            # slot sweep / remembered clear / string sweep / trim.
            #
            # ARMED ON THE MEASURED RUNS, which needs justifying because
            # it means publishing throughput from an instrumented
            # binary. Six clock reads per collection, ~36k over a cell,
            # order 1 ms against 40 s. A/B'd rather than asserted --
            # five interleaved rounds, medians within noise, response
            # byte-identical. Re-run it if the reporter ever grows a
            # cost.
            #
            # WHY NIGHTLY AND NOT ON DEMAND: it cannot be got
            # retroactively. When a number moves, THAT night's phase
            # split is what explains it, and a diagnostic run days later
            # is a different binary on a different tree. The 09-03
            # threaded halving and the tail that outlived the 09-06
            # recovery both started from "the number moved" with nothing
            # to look at.
            ( cd "$SP_EMIT" && exec env "${workers_env[@]}" \
                SPINEL_GC_STATS=1 SPINEL_GC_PHASES=1 \
                DATABASE_POOL_SIZE=64 BLOG_DB=storage/development.sqlite3 \
                PORT="$port" SECRET_KEY_BASE="$SPINEL_SECRET" \
                ./build/bin/blog ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        rust)
            # Basecamp's once-campfire-rust: one process, own front server
            # (gzip, HTTP/1) on HTTP_PORT. DISABLE_SSL so we talk plain
            # HTTP; TARGET_PORT is the loopback app listener and must not
            # collide with HTTP_PORT.
            echo "bin=$RUST_BIN" > "$shape"
            echo "workers=1 (one process)" >> "$shape"
            mkdir -p "$RUST_ROOT/storage/files"
            ( cd "$RUST_ROOT" && exec env \
                SECRET_KEY_BASE="$SPINEL_SECRET" SECRET_KEY_BASE_DUMMY=1 \
                DISABLE_SSL=1 HTTP_PORT="$port" TARGET_PORT="$((port + 2000))" \
                CAMPFIRE_STORAGE_PATH="$RUST_ROOT/storage" \
                "$RUST_BIN" server ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        tobi)
            # As its Dockerfile runs it: `ruby --yjit bin/campfire`,
            # jemalloc preloaded, WEB_CONCURRENCY worker Ractors (and as
            # many job Ractors) in one process. Rails' worker formula,
            # as once-campfire-rust's bench/run gives every app.
            local jem w; jem="$(find_jemalloc || true)"; w="$(deployed_worker_count)"
            printf 'root=%s\nrevision=%s\nruby=%s\njemalloc=%s\nworkers=%s ractors (one process)\n' \
                "$TOBI_ROOT" "$(git -C "$TOBI_ROOT" rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null)" \
                "$(ruby -e 'print RUBY_VERSION')" "${jem:-ABSENT}" "$w" > "$shape"
            mkdir -p "$TOBI_ROOT/storage/files"
            ( cd "$TOBI_ROOT" && exec env \
                SECRET_KEY_BASE="$SPINEL_SECRET" DISABLE_SSL=1 HTTP_PORT="$port" \
                WEB_CONCURRENCY="$w" JOB_CONCURRENCY="$w" \
                CAMPFIRE_STORAGE_PATH="$TOBI_ROOT/storage" ${jem:+LD_PRELOAD="$jem"} \
                bundle exec ruby --yjit bin/campfire ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        go)
            echo "bin=$GO_BIN" > "$shape"
            echo "workers=1 (one process)" >> "$shape"
            mkdir -p "$GO_ROOT/storage/files"
            ( cd "$GO_ROOT" && exec env \
                SECRET_KEY_BASE="$SPINEL_SECRET" \
                DISABLE_SSL=1 HTTP_PORT="$port" TARGET_PORT="$((port + 2000))" \
                CAMPFIRE_STORAGE_PATH="$GO_ROOT/storage" \
                "$GO_BIN" server ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
        elixir)
            echo "root=$ELIXIR_ROOT" > "$shape"
            echo "workers=BEAM" >> "$shape"
            ( cd "$ELIXIR_ROOT" && exec env \
                SECRET_KEY_BASE="$SPINEL_SECRET" \
                DATABASE_PATH="$ELIXIR_ROOT/var/production.sqlite3" \
                PORT="$port" MIX_ENV=prod REDIS_URL="${REDIS_URL:-redis://127.0.0.1:6379/0}" \
                mix run --no-halt ) > "$log" 2>&1 &
            SERVER_PID=$!
            ;;
    esac
    LANE_JOBS_PID="$(sed -n 's/^resque-pool=pid //p' "$shape" 2>/dev/null | head -1)"
    # The managed runtimes boot in seconds here too (measured 4-5 s on
    # the box), but stock Rails under TruffleRuby loads its whole gem
    # graph through the interpreter first; give those lanes the budget
    # the blog bench gives them rather than declare them dead at 60 s.
    local boot_budget=60
    case "$lane" in jruby|truffleruby) boot_budget=120 ;; rails-truffleruby) boot_budget=300 ;; esac
    wait_for_port "http://127.0.0.1:$port/" "$boot_budget" || {
        fail "$lane did not come up within ${boot_budget}s — tail of $log:"
        tail -20 "$log" >&2
        BOOT_BUDGET_USED="$boot_budget"
        return 1
    }
}

# Sign in through the lane's OWN form and echo the `Cookie:` header
# value. The two trees do not share a cookie secret, so a jar minted
# against one is a 302 against the other; this is not a detail that can
# be shared between lanes.
login_cookie() {
    local lane="$1" port="$2"
    local jar="$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt"
    rm -f "$jar"
    case "$lane" in
        rails|rails-nocache|rails-1p|rails-nocache-1p|rails-truffleruby|rails-lowering)
            scripts/campfire-oracle login --out "$ORACLE" --port "$port" --jar "$jar" >/dev/null \
                || { fail "$lane sign-in failed"; return 1; }
            ;;
        ruby|ruby-1p|ruby-lowering|spinel|spinel-1p|jruby|truffleruby|elixir)
            # Token-based lanes post the form token; the lowerer-only
            # Campfire lane is explicitly configured with
            # The disposable emitted token runtime is adjusted after
            # transpilation because header_only is not modeled yet.
            local token code
            token="$(curl -sS -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' -c "$jar" \
                     "http://127.0.0.1:$port/session/new" \
                     | grep -o 'name="authenticity_token" value="[^"]*"' | head -1 \
                     | sed 's/.*value="//; s/"$//')"
            if [[ -n "$token" ]]; then
                code="$(curl -sS -b "$jar" -c "$jar" -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \
                        -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:$port/session" \
                        --data-urlencode "email_address=user1@example.com" \
                        --data-urlencode "password=secret123456" \
                        --data-urlencode "authenticity_token=$token")"
            else
                code="$(curl -sS -b "$jar" -c "$jar" -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \
                        -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:$port/session" \
                        --data-urlencode "email_address=user1@example.com" \
                        --data-urlencode "password=secret123456")"
            fi
            [[ "$code" == "302" ]] || { fail "$lane sign-in returned $code"; return 1; }
            ;;
        rust|go|tobi)
            # Header-only forgery protection: no authenticity_token on
            # the page. Sec-Fetch-Site + Origin (with port) is enough on
            # plain HTTP; the session cookie still has to be minted by
            # posting the real form.
            local code
            curl -sS -c "$jar" -o /dev/null "http://127.0.0.1:$port/session/new"
            code="$(curl -sS -b "$jar" -c "$jar" -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \
                    -X POST "http://127.0.0.1:$port/session" \
                    -H "Sec-Fetch-Site: same-origin" \
                    -H "Origin: http://127.0.0.1:$port" \
                    --data-urlencode "email_address=user1@example.com" \
                    --data-urlencode "password=secret123456")"
            [[ "$code" == "302" ]] || { fail "$lane sign-in returned $code"; return 1; }
            ;;
    esac
    awk '/session_token|_campfire_session/ {printf "%s=%s; ", $6, $7}' "$jar"
}

parse_wrk() {
    local out="$1" req_sec p50 p99 non2xx socket_errors
    req_sec="$(grep -E '^Requests/sec:' "$out" | awk '{print $2}')"
    p50="$(awk '/Latency Distribution/{f=1;next} f && /^[[:space:]]*50%/{print $2; exit}' "$out")"
    p99="$(awk '/Latency Distribution/{f=1;next} f && /^[[:space:]]*99%/{print $2; exit}' "$out")"
    socket_errors="$(awk '/Socket errors:/ {for (i=3; i<=NF; i+=2) sum += $(i+1); print sum+0; found=1; exit} END {if (!found) print 0}' "$out")"
    # A 422 error page is cheap to serve. Counting it as throughput is
    # how the first POST cells reported thousands of rejected writes.
    non2xx="$(grep -E 'Non-2xx or 3xx responses:' "$out" | awk '{print $NF}')"
    # wrk excludes requests that time out before receiving a response.
    # A throughput figure from a cell with socket errors is therefore a
    # censored rate, not a comparable completed-request rate.
    if [[ ( -n "$non2xx" && "$non2xx" != "0" ) || "$socket_errors" != "0" ]]; then
        req_sec=null
    fi
    norm() {
        [[ -z "$1" ]] && { echo null; return; }
        awk -v v="$1" 'BEGIN {
            n = v; gsub(/[a-zA-Z]+$/, "", n)
            u = v; sub(/^[0-9.]+/, "", u)
            if (u == "us") printf "%.3f", n/1000
            else if (u == "ms") printf "%.3f", n+0
            else if (u == "s") printf "%.3f", n*1000
            else printf "null"
        }'
    }
    [[ -z "$req_sec" ]] && req_sec=null
    echo "{\"req_sec\":$req_sec,\"p50_ms\":$(norm "$p50"),\"p99_ms\":$(norm "$p99"),\"socket_errors\":$socket_errors}"
}

# ── run ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

PER_RUN="$OUT_DIR/per-run.json"
COLD="$OUT_DIR/coldstart.json"
# Set only while cold_start is booting, so those boots write their shape
# somewhere other than the measured run's record.
SHAPE_OVERRIDE=""
LOG_OVERRIDE=""
# The deployed lanes run a job runner beside the server. `stop_lane` TERMs
# the supervisor, whose EXIT trap stops it — but a supervisor that has to
# be KILLed never runs that trap, and resque-pool is then orphaned with
# its whole worker pool, holding a Redis connection and taking cores from
# the next lane. The shape record already names its pid, so stop it by
# number rather than by matching a pattern.
LANE_JOBS_PID=""
BOOT_BUDGET_USED=""
: > "$PER_RUN"
: > "$OUT_DIR/parity.txt"
: > "$COLD"

# ── cold start ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The first measure on campfire's own list, and the one no page here has
# ever carried. TWO numbers, because for a chat app they are different
# questions:
#
#   accept_ms  exec -> the port answers at all. For the deployed Rails
#              lane this is Thruster, its Puma master, and eight forked
#              workers; for the binary it is one execve.
#   page_ms    exec -> a signed-in GET of /rooms/1 comes back 200. This
#              is the honest one: it includes the first render, which on
#              a fragment-cached lane is the COLD cache (measured at
#              503 ms against a 5 ms warm read), and on ours is the only
#              render there is.
#
# The cookie is minted against a server that is then stopped, so the jar
# outlives the boot it was made in — every lane signs itself in once,
# before this, and reuses that jar here.
cold_start() {
    local lane="$1" port="$2" jar="$3" reps="${4:-3}"
    local rep t0 t_accept t_page code
    # In a SUBDIRECTORY, not `shape-coldstart-<lane>.txt`: the env.json
    # builder globs `logs/shape-*.txt` and would list each of these as a
    # lane of its own, which put eight phantom `coldstart-*` rows in the
    # published appendix.
    mkdir -p "$LOG_DIR/coldstart"
    SHAPE_OVERRIDE="$LOG_DIR/coldstart/shape-$lane.txt"
    LOG_OVERRIDE="$LOG_DIR/coldstart/$lane.log"
    for rep in $(seq 1 "$reps"); do
        t0="$(date +%s.%N)"
        if ! start_lane_quiet "$lane" "$port"; then
            fail "$lane cold-start boot $rep failed"
            stop_lane "$port"
            SHAPE_OVERRIDE=""; LOG_OVERRIDE=""
            return 0
        fi
        t_accept="$(date +%s.%N)"
        # The room page, authenticated, until it is really a 200: a lane
        # still booting answers 502, and a jar that has gone stale
        # answers 302, which would time out here rather than silently
        # recording a redirect as a rendered page.
        local deadline=$(( $(date +%s) + 120 ))
        while (( $(date +%s) < deadline )); do
            code="$(curl -s -b "$jar" -o /dev/null -w '%{http_code}' \
                    "http://127.0.0.1:$port/rooms/1" 2>/dev/null || true)"
            [[ "$code" == "200" ]] && break
            sleep 0.05
        done
        t_page="$(date +%s.%N)"
        stop_lane "$port"
        printf '{"target":"%s","rep":%d,"accept_ms":%s,"page_ms":%s,"page_code":"%s"}\n' \
            "$lane" "$rep" \
            "$(awk -v a="$t0" -v b="$t_accept" 'BEGIN{printf "%.1f", (b-a)*1000}')" \
            "$(awk -v a="$t0" -v b="$t_page" 'BEGIN{printf "%.1f", (b-a)*1000}')" \
            "$code" >> "$COLD"
    done
    SHAPE_OVERRIDE=""; LOG_OVERRIDE=""
}

# start_lane without the banner, for the cold-start reps.
start_lane_quiet() { start_lane "$@" >/dev/null 2>&1; }

stop_lane() {   # $1 = the port it was serving (optional, but pass it)
    if [[ -n "$SERVER_PID" ]]; then
        kill -TERM "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        local i
        for i in $(seq 1 100); do kill -0 "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || break; sleep 0.1; done
        kill -KILL "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        wait "$SERVER_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        SERVER_PID=""
    fi
    if [[ -n "$LANE_JOBS_PID" ]]; then
        kill -TERM "$LANE_JOBS_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        local j
        for j in $(seq 1 60); do kill -0 "$LANE_JOBS_PID" 2>/dev/null || break; sleep 0.1; done
        kill -KILL "$LANE_JOBS_PID" 2>/dev/null || true
        LANE_JOBS_PID=""
    fi
    [[ -n "${1:-}" ]] && wait_port_free "$1"
    return 0
}

port="$BASE_PORT"
for lane in "${LANES[@]}"; do
    # Say what THIS lane will actually run. The old banner printed
    # `$WORKERS` for every lane, so a deployed Rails booting eight
    # workers and a binary autodetecting twelve both announced
    # "1 worker(s)".
    case "$lane" in
        rails-nocache-1p)    shape_note="1 worker x $THREADS threads, RENDERING (no fragment cache)" ;;
        rails-lowering)     shape_note="1 worker x $THREADS threads, no fragment cache, no Thruster/resque-pool" ;;
        ruby-lowering)      shape_note="1 worker x $THREADS threads, emitted cache disabled" ;;
        rails|rails-nocache) shape_note="campfire's own worker formula x $THREADS threads, thruster, jemalloc, resque-pool" ;;
        ruby)                shape_note="$(deployed_worker_count) worker(s) x $THREADS threads, jemalloc" ;;
        spinel)              shape_note="one process, workers autodetected" ;;
        spinel-1p)           shape_note="one process, SPINEL_WORKERS=1" ;;
        rust)                shape_note="once-campfire-rust, one process, own front server" ;;
        tobi)                shape_note="campfire-once-ruby-ractor, Falcon on $(deployed_worker_count) worker Ractors, one process, jemalloc" ;;
        go)                  shape_note="once-campfire-go, one process, own front server" ;;
        elixir)              shape_note="once-campfire-elixir, Bandit, MIX_ENV=prod" ;;
        jruby)               shape_note="one process x $(jvm_threads) threads (one per hardware thread), heap $JVM_HEAP" ;;
        truffleruby)         shape_note="one process x $THREADS threads (the ruby-1p shape), heap $JVM_HEAP" ;;
        rails-truffleruby)   shape_note="one process x $THREADS threads (the rails-1p shape), heap $RAILS_TR_HEAP, no thruster, no resque-pool" ;;
        *)                   shape_note="1 worker x $THREADS threads" ;;
    esac
    step "start $lane on :$port ($shape_note)"
    if ! start_lane "$lane" "$port"; then
        fail "$lane failed to start; skipping lane"
        skip_lane "$lane" "boot" "did not answer on :$port within ${BOOT_BUDGET_USED:-60}s" "$LOG_DIR/$lane.log"
        stop_lane "$port"
        port=$(( port + 1 )); continue
    fi
    cookie="$(login_cookie "$lane" "$port")" || {
        fail "$lane sign-in failed; skipping lane"
        skip_lane "$lane" "sign-in" "the lane booted but would not sign a user in" "$LOG_DIR/$lane.log"
        stop_lane "$port"
        port=$(( port + 1 )); continue
    }
    ok "$lane up and signed in"

    # CSRF for POST cells: scrape the meta tag. Identity encoding: with
    # --gzip the overlay/tep compress HTML, and a gzip scrape would not
    # match the token regex. Empty is fine for a GET-only run; a POST
    # cell without a token is a 422 and is not a write measurement.
    csrf=""
    csrf_enc=""
    csrf_html="$LOG_DIR/csrf-$lane.html"
    # A full page, layout included: the first GET in the list, or, for a
    # POST-only list, the room the POST writes to. POST:/rooms/2/messages
    # stripped of its verb is the messages fragment, which has neither the
    # stylesheet links nor the meta tag.
    scrape_ep=""
    for scrape_e in "${ENDPOINTS[@]}"; do ep_is_post "$scrape_e" || { scrape_ep="$scrape_e"; break; }; done
    [[ -n "$scrape_ep" ]] || { scrape_ep="$(ep_path "${ENDPOINTS[0]}")"; scrape_ep="${scrape_ep%/messages}"; }
    curl -s -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' -b "$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt" \
        -o "$csrf_html" "http://127.0.0.1:$port$(ep_path "$scrape_ep")"
    csrf="$(csrf_token_from "$csrf_html")"
    BENCH_AVATAR="$(scrape_from "$csrf_html" avatar)"
    BENCH_CSS="$(scrape_from "$csrf_html" css)"
    [[ -n "$BENCH_AVATAR" && -n "$BENCH_CSS" ]] ||
        fail "$lane: room page carried no avatar or stylesheet link (avatar='$BENCH_AVATAR' css='$BENCH_CSS')"
    csrf_enc="$(python3 -c 'import urllib.parse,sys; print(urllib.parse.quote(sys.argv[1], safe=""))' "$csrf")"
    export BENCH_COOKIE="$cookie"
    export BENCH_CSRF="$csrf"
    export BENCH_CSRF_ENC="$csrf_enc"
    export BENCH_GZIP="$GZIP"

    # WARM THE FRAGMENT CACHE for the lanes that have one, deliberately
    # and on the record. The first request to a cached page is 100x the
    # rest (503 ms against 5 ms), and wrk's own warmup would absorb it
    # anyway — doing it here means the `rails` lane measures the steady
    # state it is meant to, rather than one cold render smeared across a
    # short run.
    case "$lane" in
        rails|rails-1p|rails-truffleruby|rails-lowering)
            for ep in "${ENDPOINTS[@]}"; do
                ep_is_post "$ep" && continue
                curl -s -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' -b "$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt" \
                    -o /dev/null "http://127.0.0.1:$port$(ep_path "$ep")"
            done
            ;;
    esac

    # Parity capture BEFORE the load: one authenticated GET per endpoint,
    # saved so `parity.txt` can say what each lane actually rendered. A
    # throughput number for a page that is not the same page is not a
    # comparison, and this is the cheapest possible record of that.
    # POST cells have no body to tally — they mutate the seed.
    for ep in "${ENDPOINTS[@]}"; do
        ep_is_post "$ep" && continue
        body="$LOG_DIR/body-$lane-$(ep_parity_name "$ep").html"
        code="$(curl -s -H 'Accept-Encoding: identity' -b "$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt" \
                -o "$body" -w '%{http_code}' \
                "http://127.0.0.1:$port$(ep_path "$ep")")"
        read -r tags body_sha256 < <(ruby -rdigest -e 'html = File.read(ARGV[0]); puts html.scan(/<([a-zA-Z][-a-zA-Z0-9]*)/).size, Digest::SHA256.file(ARGV[0]).hexdigest' "$body")
        printf '%s\t%s\tstatus=%s\ttags=%s\tbytes=%s\tsha256=%s\n' \
            "$lane" "$ep" "$code" "$tags" "$(wc -c < "$body" | tr -d ' ')" "$body_sha256" \
            >> "$OUT_DIR/parity.txt"
    done

    # The managed runtimes plateau later than 20 s. Floors, not a bigger
    # global default, so the other lanes do not pay for these; the
    # values are the blog bench's, set where each curve went flat ON
    # THIS BOX (scripts/bench: truffleruby 120, rails-truffleruby 240,
    # the JVM 60). `--warmup` above a floor still wins, and every row
    # records the value it got so the page can say so.
    warm="$WARMUP"
    case "$lane" in
        jruby)             (( warm < 60 ))  && warm=60 ;;
        truffleruby)       (( warm < 120 )) && warm=120 ;;
        rails-truffleruby) (( warm < 240 )) && warm=240 ;;
    esac
    wrote=0
    gzip_note=""
    (( GZIP )) && gzip_note=", gzip"
    for ep in "${ENDPOINTS[@]}"; do
        if [[ "$lane" == "rails-lowering" || "$lane" == "ruby-lowering" ]] \
            && ep_is_post "$ep"; then
            continue
        fi
        path="$(ep_path "$ep")"
        url="http://127.0.0.1:$port$path"
        if ep_is_post "$ep"; then
            case "$lane" in
                rust|go|tobi) ;;
                *)
                    if [[ -z "$csrf" ]]; then
                        fail "$lane POST skipped: no CSRF token (a 422 is not a write measurement)"
                        continue
                    fi
                    ;;
            esac
            wrk_script=("$POST_LUA")
            wrote=1
        else
            wrk_script=("$GET_LUA")
        fi
        for run in $(seq 1 "$RUNS"); do
            step "measure $lane $ep run=$run/$RUNS (warmup ${warm}s${gzip_note})"
            wrk -t"$WRK_THREADS" -c"$CONNECTIONS" -d"${warm}s" \
                -s "${wrk_script[0]}" "$url" >/dev/null 2>&1 || true
            wrk_out="$LOG_DIR/wrk-$lane-$(ep_parity_name "$ep")-$run.txt"
            rss_out="$LOG_DIR/rss-$lane-$(ep_parity_name "$ep")-$run.txt"
            : > "$rss_out"
            # Two columns per sample: RSS sum, then PSS sum (empty
            # where the kernel has no smaps_rollup).
            ( while server_alive; do
                  echo "$(sum_rss_kb "$SERVER_PID") $(sum_pss_kb "$SERVER_PID")" >> "$rss_out"
                  sleep 1
              done ) &
            rss_pid=$!
            wrk -t"$WRK_THREADS" -c"$CONNECTIONS" -d"${DURATION}s" --latency \
                -s "${wrk_script[0]}" "$url" > "$wrk_out" 2>&1 || true
            kill "$rss_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
            wait "$rss_pid" 2>/dev/null || true
            rss="$(awk '{print $1}' "$rss_out" | sort -n | awk '{a[NR]=$1} END {print NR ? a[int((NR+1)/2)] : 0}')"
            pss="$(awk 'NF>1 {print $2}' "$rss_out" | sort -n | awk '{a[NR]=$1} END {print NR ? a[int((NR+1)/2)] : ""}')"
            printf '{"target":"%s","workers":%d,"endpoint":"%s","run":%d,"warmup_sec":%d,"rss_kb":%s,"pss_kb":%s,%s}\n' \
                "$lane" "$WORKERS" "$ep" "$run" "$warm" "${rss:-0}" "${pss:-null}" \
                "$(parse_wrk "$wrk_out" | sed 's/^{//;s/}$//')" >> "$PER_RUN"
            # AFTER the row, not before. A cell whose server died still has
            # to be written — with the null req_sec parse_wrk gives for a
            # refused connection — because that is how bench-report tells
            # "stopped partway" from "never ran": it compares rows attempted
            # against rows that carry a number. Skipping the row instead
            # makes a dead lane look complete but short, and the banner
            # never fires. skipped.json then supplies the reason the row
            # cannot: which signal.
            reap_server "$lane" "measure $ep run=$run/$RUNS" || break 2
        done
    done

    stop_lane "$port"

    # Cold start, after the load and with this lane's jar in hand. Redis
    # is NOT flushed between reps, deliberately: restarting a campfire
    # container does not empty the cache its own Procfile keeps in a
    # separate process, so a warm Redis is what their restart actually
    # sees. Rep 1 still pays a cold render for anything the load did not
    # touch. Skipped on --http-suite: that flag is the HTTP table, not a
    # second copy of the cold-start section.
    if (( HTTP_SUITE == 0 )); then
        step "cold start $lane (3 boots)"
        cold_start "$lane" "$port" "$LOG_DIR/jar-$lane.txt" 3
    fi

    # AFTER the cold start, not before it: the jar's session is a row in
    # the database this lane signed in against, and the restaged seed has
    # no such row, so every cold-start boot answered 302 to the login page
    # until it timed out (10-07, the first nightly with POST in the default
    # suite). The writes went to the POST route's room, not /rooms/1.
    if (( wrote )); then
        step "restaging the seed after $lane writes"
        restore_seeded_db
    fi

    port=$(( port + 1 ))
done

# ── the WebSocket phase ──────────────────────────────────────────────
#
# The measure campfire's own discussion calls the one that matters most —
# "how many concurrent WebSocket connections a given box will hold" — and
# the one this page has never carried, because it lives in a different
# harness. It still does: `scripts/campfire-cable-sweep` drives both
# lanes with one client, and duplicating any of it here would be a second
# implementation of the thing that makes the comparison honest. This runs
# it and folds its results in.
#
# PINNED TO FOUR CORES, which is once.com/campfire's own 1,000-user tier
# (4 CPU / 8 GB). Unpinned it would measure this box instead of a tier,
# and campfire's puma.rb would size its worker count to the whole
# machine while the row claimed to be a tier.
run_socket_phase() {
    local sweep="$REPO_ROOT/scripts/campfire-cable-sweep"
    [[ -x "$sweep" ]] || { fail "no campfire-cable-sweep — skipping the socket phase"; return 0; }

    # THE TIER GETS ITS OWN DATASET. The HTTP seed is 50 users in 5
    # rooms, deliberately — those pages have to render in a comparable
    # time. Pointing a 1,000-socket sweep at it produces 5 rooms of 200
    # subscribers sharing 50 cookies twenty ways, and since one message
    # costs one write per subscriber in its room, that measures a room
    # size nobody deploys. `socket-seed` builds 1,000 users over 20 rooms
    # once, caches it under build/, and restores the oracle afterwards.
    local db cookies
    if [[ -n "$SOCKET_DB" ]]; then
        db="$SOCKET_DB"
        cookies="${SOCKET_COOKIES:-$ORACLE/tmp/session-cookies.txt}"
    else
        db="$REPO_ROOT/build/campfire-socket-seed.sqlite3"
        cookies="${SOCKET_COOKIES:-$REPO_ROOT/build/campfire-socket-cookies.txt}"
        scripts/campfire-oracle socket-seed --out "$ORACLE" --app "$APP" \
            --seed-db "$db" --seed-cookies "$cookies" \
            || { fail "could not build the socket seed — skipping the socket phase"; return 0; }
    fi
    [[ -f "$db" ]] || { fail "no socket database at $db — skipping the socket phase"; return 0; }
    [[ -s "$cookies" ]] || { fail "no cookie file at $cookies — skipping the socket phase"; return 0; }

    local per_room="$SOCKET_PER_ROOM"
    (( per_room < 1 )) && per_room=1

    # campfire's own formula at the tier's core count, which is what its
    # puma.rb would compute inside a 4-CPU container.
    local tier_cpus rails_workers
    tier_cpus="$(awk -F- '{ if (NF==2) print $2-$1+1; else print 1 }' <<< "$SOCKET_CPUS")"
    rails_workers="$(ruby -e 'print (ARGV[0].to_i * 0.666).ceil' "$tier_cpus")"

    if [[ -x "$SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog" ]]; then
        step "socket phase: the binary, $SOCKET_SIZES sockets on cpus $SOCKET_CPUS"
        "$sweep" --lane binary --bin "$SP_EMIT/build/bin/blog" \
            --db "$db" --cookies "$cookies" --cpus "$SOCKET_CPUS" \
            --workers "$tier_cpus" --sizes "$SOCKET_SIZES" --per-room "$per_room" \
            --secret "$SPINEL_SECRET" --out "$OUT_DIR/sockets-binary" \
            > "$LOG_DIR/sockets-binary.log" 2>&1 \
            || fail "the binary's socket lane failed (see $LOG_DIR/sockets-binary.log)"
    else
        fail "no spinel binary — the socket phase has no binary lane"
    fi

    step "socket phase: rails, $SOCKET_SIZES sockets on cpus $SOCKET_CPUS ($rails_workers workers)"
    "$sweep" --lane rails --oracle "$ORACLE" \
        --db "$db" --cookies "$cookies" --cpus "$SOCKET_CPUS" \
        --workers "$rails_workers" --threads "$THREADS" \
        --sizes "$SOCKET_SIZES" --per-room "$per_room" \
        --out "$OUT_DIR/sockets-rails" \
        > "$LOG_DIR/sockets-rails.log" 2>&1 \
        || fail "the rails socket lane failed (see $LOG_DIR/sockets-rails.log)"

    # THE SAME cable.rb AS THE BINARY, ON CRuby — which is the only way
    # to say whether this design's cost is the DESIGN or the COMPILER.
    # Measured 2026-09-07: idle 0.009 cores against the binary's 0.011
    # and Rails' 0.0125, delivering 0.044 against 0.041 and 0.157. The
    # two lanes running our reactor are indistinguishable on both axes
    # and Rails is ~3.6x dearer to deliver on, so the win is the design.
    # What the binary adds is memory (141 vs 199 MB) and tail (84 vs
    # 119 ms p99).
    #
    # ONE WORKER, and `campfire-cable-sweep` refuses anything else on
    # this lane: `Cable::Registry` is per-process and cross-worker
    # broadcast needs a transport behind `Broadcasts.set_transport`,
    # which is not implemented. So this row is one process against the
    # binary's $tier_cpus workers and Rails' $rails_workers — the
    # per-connection columns (idle, delivering, per-frame) are the fair
    # comparison; the totals are not.
    if [[ -d "$EMIT" ]]; then
        step "socket phase: ruby, $SOCKET_SIZES sockets on cpus $SOCKET_CPUS (1 worker)"
        "$sweep" --lane ruby --emit "$EMIT" \
            --db "$db" --cookies "$cookies" --cpus "$SOCKET_CPUS" \
            --workers 1 --threads "$THREADS" \
            --sizes "$SOCKET_SIZES" --per-room "$per_room" \
            --secret "$SPINEL_SECRET" --out "$OUT_DIR/sockets-ruby" \
            > "$LOG_DIR/sockets-ruby.log" 2>&1 \
            || fail "the ruby socket lane failed (see $LOG_DIR/sockets-ruby.log)"
    fi

    # One file for the renderer, lanes labelled the way the HTTP rows are.
    ruby - "$OUT_DIR/sockets-binary/results.jsonl" "$OUT_DIR/sockets-rails/results.jsonl" \
           "$OUT_DIR/sockets-ruby/results.jsonl" "$OUT_DIR/sockets.json" <<'RUBY'
require "json"
binary, rails, emit_ruby, out = ARGV
rows = []
# Order is the page's: our two lanes, then theirs.
{ "spinel" => binary, "ruby" => emit_ruby, "rails" => rails }.each do |lane, path|
  next unless File.exist?(path)
  File.readlines(path, chomp: true).reject(&:empty?).each do |line|
    d = JSON.parse(line) rescue next
    rows << {
      "target" => lane, "sockets" => d["sockets"],
      "subscribers_per_room" => d["subscribers_per_room"],
      "storm_s" => d.dig("storm", "seconds"), "storm_cpu_s" => d.dig("storm", "cpu_seconds"),
      "failed" => d.dig("storm", "failed"),
      "idle_cores" => d.dig("idle", "cpu_cores"),
      "rss_mb" => d.dig("idle", "rss_mb"), "pss_mb" => d.dig("idle", "pss_mb"),
      "pings" => d.dig("idle", "pings"),
      "delivered" => d.dig("chat", "delivered"), "expected" => d.dig("chat", "expected"),
      "frame_p50_ms" => d.dig("chat", "frame_p50_ms"), "frame_p99_ms" => d.dig("chat", "frame_p99_ms"),
      "chat_cores" => d.dig("chat", "cpu_cores"),
      "driver_max_pass_ms" => d.dig("chat", "driver_max_pass_ms"),
      "teardown_cpu_s" => d.dig("teardown", "cpu_seconds"),
      "server" => d["server"],
    }
  end
end
File.write(out, JSON.pretty_generate(rows) + "\n") unless rows.empty?
RUBY
    [[ -f "$OUT_DIR/sockets.json" ]] && ok "socket phase -> $OUT_DIR/sockets.json"
    return 0
}
(( SOCKETS )) && run_socket_phase

# ── collector, per spinel lane ───────────────────────────────────────
#
# One file rather than a column on summary.json, and the reason is the
# shape of the data: ONE server process serves all three runs of BOTH
# endpoints, so `[gc]` is cumulative across the lane and cannot be
# attributed to a row. Folding it into the rows would duplicate it and
# imply an attribution that is not there. Same reasoning that gives
# sockets.json and coldstart.json their own files.
#
# Cumulative-per-lane is enough for what this is for -- "which phase
# moved since last night" -- and per-cell deltas (what the scale ladder
# takes) would be a bigger apparatus for a question nobody has asked
# yet. The limitation is recorded in the file rather than left implicit.
ruby - "$LOG_DIR" "$OUT_DIR/gc.json" <<'RUBY'
require "json"
log_dir, out = ARGV
rows = []
Dir.glob(File.join(log_dir, "spinel*.log")).sort.each do |path|
  lane = File.basename(path, ".log")
  lines = File.readlines(path, chomp: true) rescue next
  # THE LINE WITH THE MOST COLLECTIONS, not the last one. Belt to
  # LOG_OVERRIDE's braces: if anything ever boots a lane twice into one
  # log again, "last" silently reports the short-lived process (the
  # first nightly with this capture said "1 collections, 0.0s" for a
  # lane that had served thousands of requests) while "most" still finds
  # the run that did the work. Cumulative counters only rise, so the
  # maximum IS the longest-lived process's final word.
  gc  = lines.select { |l| l.start_with?("[gc] ") }
              .max_by { |l| l[/\[gc\] (\d+)/, 1].to_i }
  gcp = lines.select { |l| l.start_with?("[gcph] ") }
              .max_by { |l| l[/of ([\d.]+)s total/, 1].to_f }
  next unless gc || gcp
  row = { "target" => lane, "note" => "cumulative over the whole lane: all runs of both endpoints, one process" }
  if gc
    row["gc_line"] = gc
    if (m = gc.match(/\[gc\] (\d+) collections \((\d+) full\) in ([\d.]+)s of ([\d.]+)s wall \(([\d.]+)%\), ([\d.]+)ms avg/))
      row.merge!("collections" => m[1].to_i, "full_collections" => m[2].to_i,
                 "gc_seconds" => m[3].to_f, "wall_seconds" => m[4].to_f,
                 "gc_pct_of_wall" => m[5].to_f, "avg_ms" => m[6].to_f)
    end
    if (m = gc.match(/live ([\d.]+) MB obj \+ ([\d.]+) MB str/))
      row.merge!("live_obj_mb" => m[1].to_f, "live_str_mb" => m[2].to_f)
    end
  end
  if gcp
    row["gcph_line"] = gcp
    # Phase names are the collector's own words (matz/spinel#4382), so a
    # number here leads to the code that spent it without a translation.
    { "mark" => /mark ([\d.]+)s/, "old_sweep" => /old sweep ([\d.]+)s/,
      "slot_sweep" => /slot sweep ([\d.]+)s/, "remembered_clear" => /remembered clear ([\d.]+)s/,
      "string_sweep" => /string sweep ([\d.]+)s/, "trim" => /trim ([\d.]+)s/,
      "phase_total" => /of ([\d.]+)s total/ }.each do |k, re|
      row[k] = gcp[re, 1].to_f if gcp =~ re
    end
  end
  rows << row
end
File.write(out, JSON.pretty_generate(rows) + "\n") unless rows.empty?
RUBY
[[ -f "$OUT_DIR/gc.json" ]] && ok "collector -> $OUT_DIR/gc.json"

# ── provenance ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# The machine-and-toolchain half is scripts/bench-env — the SAME capture
# the blog bench publishes, so the two pages' appendices are comparable
# and neither is a hand-rolled second copy free to drift. A hand-rolled
# one already cost a whole run's page: it wrote `"host": "<uname>"` where
# bench-report reads a map, and the report aborted on it.
#
# Campfire's own half is merged on top. It is the half a reader needs
# most and the half no generic capture can know: WHICH campfire (the app
# SHA) and WHICH Rails (campfire's Gemfile tracks rails/rails main, so
# the framework is pinned only by the lockfile's git revision), plus the
# seed size and whether the fragment cache was on. campfire-oracle
# writes those into oracle.json at prepare time.
"$REPO_ROOT/scripts/bench-env" \
    --workers     "$WORKERS" \
    --concurrency "$CONNECTIONS" \
    --runs        "$RUNS" \
    --duration    "$DURATION" \
    --warmup      "$WARMUP" \
    --threads     "$THREADS" \
    --wrk-threads "$WRK_THREADS" \
    --endpoints   "${ENDPOINTS[*]}" \
    --targets     "${LANES[*]}" \
    --command     "scripts/bench-campfire ${ORIG_ARGS[*]}" \
    > "$OUT_DIR/env.base.json" 2>/dev/null || echo '{}' > "$OUT_DIR/env.base.json"

ruby - "$OUT_DIR/env.base.json" "$ORACLE/oracle.json" "$OUT_DIR/env.json" \
       "$ORACLE/Gemfile.lock" "$EMIT/Gemfile.lock" "$LATEST_CAMPFIRE" <<'RUBY'
require "json"

base_path, oracle_path, out_path, rails_lock, emit_lock, latest_campfire = ARGV

def read_json(path)
  JSON.parse(File.read(path))
rescue Errno::ENOENT, JSON::ParserError
  nil
end

env = read_json(base_path) || {}
env["benchmark"] = "campfire"
oracle = read_json(oracle_path)
env["oracle"] = oracle if oracle
env["campfire_source_mode"] = latest_campfire == "1" ?
  "latest basecamp/once-campfire main snapshot" : "explicit --app checkout"

# The lane note travels WITH the data, because the renderer is generic
# and the only place a sentence about THESE lanes can live is here,
# beside the run it describes.
# The app server is part of the measurement and the two lanes resolve it
# separately — campfire's lockfile pins one, the emitted Gemfile asks for
# whatever is current. Their exact versions are attached below.
def puma_version(lock_path)
  File.read(lock_path)[/^\s+puma \(([^)]+)\)/, 1]
rescue Errno::ENOENT
  nil
end

servers = {
  "rails lanes" => puma_version(rails_lock)&.then { |v| "puma #{v}" },
  "emit lanes"  => puma_version(emit_lock)&.then { |v| "puma #{v}" },
}.compact
env["servers"] = servers unless servers.empty?

# The note is CONDITIONAL on the nocache lane actually running. It used
# to be unconditional because that lane was always published; now it is a
# diagnostic, and a page that explains a column it does not carry is
# noise.
#
# `targets` lives under `harness` (scripts/bench-env writes
# `harness[:targets]`), NOT at the top level — a top-level read returns
# nil, which reads as "not requested" and would silently keep the note
# off the page on the very run that needed it.
targets_ran = Array(env.dig("harness", "targets"))
nocache_ran = targets_ran.include?("rails-nocache")
jvm_ran     = (targets_ran & %w[jruby truffleruby]).any?
tr_ran      = targets_ran.include?("truffleruby")
rails_tr_ran = targets_ran.include?("rails-truffleruby")
one_p_ran   = targets_ran.any? { |t| t.end_with?("-1p") }
tobi_ran    = targets_ran.include?("tobi")

env["notes"] = [
  "Current Campfire's full-response, fragment/presentation, and record " \
  "caches are bounded process-local stores, separate from its configured " \
  "Rails.cache Redis adapter. This report is restricted to renderer-only " \
  "lanes; cache-on deployment comparisons are not produced by this " \
  "cache-off harness.",
  ("<code>rails-nocache</code> is Rails RENDERING, with " \
   "<code>perform_caching = false</code>. It is a legacy diagnostic — campfire's " \
   "messages index preloads only <code>with_creator</code>, so boosts, " \
   "rich text and attachments are N+1 in Rails too." if nocache_ran),
  ("<code>rails-lowering</code> and <code>ruby-lowering</code> are the " \
   "GET-only renderer-focused pair: both lanes use the current upstream " \
   "source plus the identical documented campfire-lowering-v6 cache-off " \
   "transform. One Puma worker, full-response, " \
   "fragment/presentation, and record-snapshot caches disabled on both " \
   "sides, and no Thruster/resque-pool on Rails; the production cache store " \
   "is also replaced by Rails' null store. Both use the same Ruby and Puma " \
   "versions. The disposable source transform disables forgery protection " \
   "in Rails, and the emitted tree's disposable token runtime is adjusted the same " \
   "way, because its runtime does not yet model Campfire's header-only CSRF " \
   "strategy. This keeps form markup comparable but is not " \
   "a security or deployment comparison. POST is " \
   "excluded; Redis-backed job and cable adapters remain configured but " \
   "are not part of these GET requests." \
   if targets_ran.include?("rails-lowering") && targets_ran.include?("ruby-lowering")),
  "The deployed lanes are what each project would run on this box. " \
  "<code>rails</code> is once-campfire AS ITS OWN DOCKERFILE RUNS IT: " \
  "Thruster in front of Puma, <code>(processor_count * 0.666).ceil</code> " \
  "workers x 5 threads from its own <code>config/puma.rb</code>, jemalloc " \
  "preloaded, resque-pool alongside. <code>ruby</code> is the CRuby emit " \
  "in the same worker/thread shape with the same allocator. The renderer " \
  "pair pins both Puma lanes to Campfire's lockfile version. " \
  "<code>spinel</code> is " \
  "the binary with its workers autodetected." \
  if (targets_ran & %w[rails ruby spinel]).any?,
  "<code>ruby</code> IS A MATCHED-SHAPE RUNTIME COMPARISON, NOT A " \
  "DEPLOYMENT CLAIM. It runs Rails' worker count so the number is not a " \
  "core count, but the emitted tree's Action Cable registry and its job " \
  "queue are both in-process: at more than one worker a broadcast reaches " \
  "only the subscribers sharing a process with the poster, so campfire " \
  "could not actually run this shape. Its deployable CRuby shape today is " \
  "one worker; one process serving every core (Falcon on Ractors, the " \
  "shape <code>spinel</code> already has) is the planned replacement. " \
  "<code>spinel</code> needs none of this: " \
  "one process, green threads, one shared registry — which is why it is " \
  "the emit lane the WebSocket tier can measure at its full worker count." \
  if (targets_ran & %w[ruby spinel]).any?,
  ("The <code>-1p</code> lanes are the diagnostic, and what they pin is " \
  "ONE CPU rather than one process — CRuby needs a process per core " \
  "because of the GVL, while the binary is a single process either way " \
  "and varies its OS worker count instead (<code>SPINEL_WORKERS=1</code>). " \
  "They exist because a deployed number cannot say WHY it landed where it " \
  "did, so these rows separate \"their runtime is slower\" from \"we gave " \
  "them fewer cores\". Read them per lane-family: <code>ruby</code> and " \
  "<code>spinel</code> differ from their <code>-1p</code> twins in worker " \
  "count and NOTHING else, but <code>rails-1p</code> also sheds Thruster " \
  "and resque-pool, so its delta is not attributable to cores alone." if one_p_ran),
  "Known omissions, stated rather than discovered: the WebSocket phase's " \
  "rails lane runs Puma with campfire's worker count and its allocator but " \
  "WITHOUT Thruster and resque-pool, and no lane terminates TLS." \
  if (targets_ran & %w[rails ruby spinel]).any?,
  "MEMORY IS THE SERVER PROCESS TREE, and the rails lanes keep part of " \
  "themselves outside it. Action Cable and Resque adapters may use system " \
  "Redis, which is nobody's descendant and so is outside lane RSS; " \
  "Campfire's page caches are process-local and are counted in Rails' RSS. " \
  "Throughput includes any Redis round trips made by those adapters. " \
  "Counted for rails: Thruster, the Puma master and its workers, " \
  "resque-pool and its workers." \
  if (targets_ran & %w[rails ruby spinel]).any?,
  ("<code>tobi</code> is <a href=\"https://github.com/tobi/campfire-once-ruby-ractor\">" \
   "tobi/campfire-once-ruby-ractor</a>: campfire ported BY HAND to plain " \
   "Ruby, no Rails, served by Falcon from worker Ractors in one process, " \
   "with in-process job and cable Ractors. It is the measured answer to " \
   "\"how fast can careful Ruby serve this app\", and the target for the " \
   "emitted <code>ruby</code> lane. It reads the same seeded database. " \
   "Its deliberate differences from Rails (header-only CSRF, so its " \
   "pages carry no tokens; part-hashed ETags; fewer cookie writes) are " \
   "listed in its README." if tobi_ran),
  "The CRuby fragment store is PER PROCESS, so the <code>ruby</code> " \
  "lane holds one cache per worker where <code>spinel</code> holds one " \
  "for the whole binary." if (targets_ran & %w[ruby spinel]).any?,
  ("<code>jruby</code> and <code>truffleruby</code> are the SAME emitted " \
   "tree under another Ruby — <code>jruby</code> re-emitted with " \
   "<code>--target jruby</code> (identical app and framework; SQLite over " \
   "JDBC instead of the C extension), <code>truffleruby</code> the " \
   "<code>ruby</code> tree unmodified with a bundle TruffleRuby compiled " \
   "itself. Both are ONE process, because neither can fork: " \
   "<code>jruby</code> with a Puma thread per hardware thread (no GVL — " \
   "measured on this box, 12 threads beat 40 by 19%), " \
   "<code>truffleruby</code> in the <code>ruby-1p</code> shape of one " \
   "process × 5 threads (it keeps a GIL for Ruby code, and 4 threads " \
   "beat 40 by 56%). That makes <code>jruby</code> the one " \
   "multi-core CRuby-family row campfire could actually deploy: the JVM " \
   "has no GVL, and one process means one Action Cable registry, one " \
   "fragment cache and one job queue shared by every thread — the exact " \
   "property the eight-worker <code>ruby</code> row lacks. Heaps are " \
   "pinned at 1 GB (512 MB, the blog's budget, holds the tree but costs " \
   "it 24% to GC pressure)." if jvm_ran),
  ("<code>truffleruby</code> reads the other way from the blog page, and " \
   "the cause is the GEM, not the runtime. TruffleRuby has no GVL for " \
   "Ruby code; it gives a GLOBAL C-EXTENSION LOCK to any extension that " \
   "has not declared itself thread-safe, and <code>sqlite3</code> 2.9.3 " \
   "declares neither <code>rb_ext_ractor_safe()</code> nor " \
   "<code>rb_ext_thread_safe()</code> — so every row every thread steps " \
   "queues behind every other thread's. This lane runs the DEFAULT, " \
   "locked, because that is what <code>bundle install</code> gives a " \
   "deployer; <code>--cexts-lock=false</code>, TruffleRuby's own " \
   "documented benchmarking advice, is worth 2.5× on this page (432 → " \
   "1,078 req/s at 5 threads, 393 → 977 at 12, measured 2026-09-22), " \
   "which puts the emit at 2.3× the CRuby one-process row rather than " \
   "0.9×. What the flag does NOT remove is the per-call cost: " \
   "single-threaded, a row stepped through the gem is 2.4 µs here " \
   "against CRuby's 0.83, and the lock is only ~3% of that. Read this " \
   "row against <code>ruby-1p</code>, the same one-process shape on " \
   "CRuby." if tr_ran),
  ("<code>rails-truffleruby</code> is once-campfire ITSELF under " \
   "TruffleRuby: the same oracle tree, rows, production config and " \
   "process-local full-response/fragment caches (not Redis), with a second " \
   "bundle whose C extensions TruffleRuby " \
   "compiled. It is ONE Puma process × 5 threads — TruffleRuby has no " \
   "<code>fork</code>, so neither campfire's worker formula nor " \
   "resque-pool can run, and Thruster is not in front — which makes it a " \
   "runtime swap on the one-process <code>rails-1p</code> row, not on " \
   "<code>rails</code>. Heap " \
   "pinned at 2 GB, twice the blog's stock-Rails budget, for twice the " \
   "working set. There is no <code>rails-jruby</code>: the AR-JDBC adapter " \
   "drives Rails 8.0 only and campfire tracks rails/rails main, a gap " \
   "recorded here rather than shimmed with a hand-pinned app." if rails_tr_ran),
]
env["notes"] = env["notes"].compact

# WHAT EACH LANE ACTUALLY GOT. A deployed lane that claimed jemalloc and
# ran without it would be the exact failure this shape is meant to end,
# so the achieved parts travel with the data rather than the intent.
shapes = {}
Dir[File.join(File.dirname(out_path), "logs", "shape-*.txt")].sort.each do |f|
  lane = File.basename(f, ".txt").sub(/\Ashape-/, "")
  parts = File.readlines(f, chomp: true).reject(&:empty?)
  shapes[lane] = parts unless parts.empty?
end
env["lane_shapes"] = shapes unless shapes.empty?

File.write(out_path, JSON.pretty_generate(env) + "\n")
RUBY
rm -f "$OUT_DIR/env.base.json"

# ── summarize ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Same shape scripts/bench writes, so bench-report renders either.

ruby - "$PER_RUN" "$OUT_DIR/summary.json" "$OUT_DIR/summary.md" "$OUT_DIR/parity.txt" <<'RUBY'
require "json"
per_run, sj, sm, parity = ARGV
records = File.readlines(per_run, chomp: true).reject(&:empty?).map { |l| JSON.parse(l) }
abort "no measurements" if records.empty?
parity_rows = {}
if File.file?(parity)
  File.readlines(parity, chomp: true).each do |line|
    lane, endpoint, status, tags, bytes, sha256 = line.split("\t")
    parity_rows[[lane, endpoint]] = {
      status: status.to_s.split("=", 2).last,
      tags: tags.to_s.split("=", 2).last,
      bytes: bytes.to_s.split("=", 2).last,
      sha256: sha256.to_s.split("=", 2).last
    }
  end
end

def median(xs)
  xs = xs.compact.sort
  return nil if xs.empty?
  xs.size.odd? ? xs[xs.size / 2] : (xs[xs.size / 2 - 1] + xs[xs.size / 2]) / 2.0
end

cells = records.group_by { |r| [r["target"], r["workers"], r["endpoint"]] }.map do |(t, w, e), runs|
  rss_kb = median(runs.map { |r| r["rss_kb"] }.reject { |v| v.to_i.zero? })
  pss_kb = median(runs.map { |r| r["pss_kb"] }.compact.reject { |v| v.to_i.zero? })
  req = median(runs.map { |r| r["req_sec"] })
  { target: t, workers: w, endpoint: e, runs: runs.size,
    req_sec: req, p50_ms: median(runs.map { |r| r["p50_ms"] }),
    p99_ms: median(runs.map { |r| r["p99_ms"] }),
    rss_kb: rss_kb, rss_mb: rss_kb && rss_kb / 1024.0,
    pss_kb: pss_kb, pss_mb: pss_kb && pss_kb / 1024.0,
    # Per-GB against the FAIR number: an over-counted denominator makes
    # a multi-process lane look more efficient than it is... and a
    # single-process one look worse.
    rps_per_gb: (req && (pss_kb || rss_kb) && (pss_kb || rss_kb) > 0) ?
                 req / ((pss_kb || rss_kb) / 1024.0 / 1024.0) : nil }
end
File.write(sj, JSON.pretty_generate(cells))

md = +"# campfire: Rails vs the emitted tree\n\n"
md << "_#{records.size} measurements across #{cells.size} cells_\n\n"
if cells.any? { |c| c[:target] == "rails-lowering" } &&
   cells.any? { |c| c[:target] == "ruby-lowering" }
  unmatched = cells.select { |c| c[:target] == "rails-lowering" }.filter_map do |rails_cell|
    ruby_cell = cells.find do |candidate|
      candidate[:target] == "ruby-lowering" && candidate[:endpoint] == rails_cell[:endpoint]
    end
    rails_shape = parity_rows[["rails-lowering", rails_cell[:endpoint]]]
    ruby_shape = parity_rows[["ruby-lowering", rails_cell[:endpoint]]]
    rails_cell[:endpoint] unless ruby_cell && rails_shape && ruby_shape &&
      rails_shape[:status] == "200" && ruby_shape[:status] == "200" &&
      rails_shape[:tags] == ruby_shape[:tags] && rails_shape[:bytes] == ruby_shape[:bytes] &&
      !rails_shape[:sha256].empty? && rails_shape[:sha256] == ruby_shape[:sha256]
  end
  unless unmatched.empty?
    md << "**No valid Rails-vs-emitted speedup is reported:** the pre-load response check " \
          "(HTTP status, HTML tag count, byte count, and full-body SHA-256) differs for " \
          "#{unmatched.map { |ep| "`#{ep}`" }.join(", ")}. The rates below are diagnostic only; " \
          "they do not establish equivalent output.\n\n"
  end
end
cell = ->(v, p) { v.nil? ? "—" : ("%.#{p}f" % v) }
cells.group_by { |c| c[:endpoint] }.each do |ep, eg|
  md << "## `#{ep}`\n\n"
  md << "| lane | req/sec | p50 (ms) | p99 (ms) | RSS (MB) | req/sec/GB |\n"
  md << "|------|--------:|---------:|---------:|---------:|-----------:|\n"
  eg.sort_by { |c| c[:target] }.each do |c|
    md << "| %s | %s | %s | %s | %s | %s |\n" % [
      c[:target], cell.(c[:req_sec], 1), cell.(c[:p50_ms], 2),
      cell.(c[:p99_ms], 2), cell.(c[:rss_mb], 0), cell.(c[:rps_per_gb], 0) ]
  end
  # The ratio, stated rather than left to the reader — and only when both
  # operands were measured in this run.
  ruby = eg.find { |c| c[:target] == "ruby" }
  if ruby && ruby[:req_sec] && ruby[:req_sec] > 0
    rails = eg.find { |c| c[:target] == "rails" }
    if rails && rails[:req_sec] && rails[:req_sec] > 0
      md << "\n_Deployment: ruby / rails = %.2fx_\n" % (ruby[:req_sec] / rails[:req_sec])
    end
  end
  ruby_lowering = eg.find { |c| c[:target] == "ruby-lowering" }
  rails_lowering = eg.find { |c| c[:target] == "rails-lowering" }
  rails_shape = parity_rows[["rails-lowering", ep]]
  ruby_shape = parity_rows[["ruby-lowering", ep]]
  parity_ok = rails_shape && ruby_shape && rails_shape[:status] == "200" &&
    ruby_shape[:status] == "200" && rails_shape[:tags] == ruby_shape[:tags] &&
    rails_shape[:bytes] == ruby_shape[:bytes] && !rails_shape[:sha256].empty? &&
    rails_shape[:sha256] == ruby_shape[:sha256]
  if parity_ok && ruby_lowering&.dig(:req_sec).to_f > 0 && rails_lowering&.dig(:req_sec).to_f > 0
    md << "\n_Renderer comparison (fragment cache off): ruby-lowering / rails-lowering = %.2fx_\n" %
          (ruby_lowering[:req_sec] / rails_lowering[:req_sec])
  end
  md << "\n"
end

if File.exist?(parity) && !File.read(parity).empty?
  md << "## What each lane rendered\n\n"
  md << "Pre-load response audit: one authenticated GET per lane per endpoint.\n\n"
  md << "| lane | endpoint | status | tags | bytes | body SHA-256 (prefix) |\n|---|---|--:|--:|--:|---|\n"
  File.readlines(parity, chomp: true).each do |line|
    lane, ep, st, tags, bytes, sha256 = line.split("\t")
    md << "| %s | `%s` | %s | %s | %s | `%s` |\n" % [lane, ep, st.sub("status=", ""),
                                                        tags.sub("tags=", ""), bytes.sub("bytes=", ""), sha256.to_s.sub("sha256=", "")[0, 12]]
  end
  md << "\n"
end
File.write(sm, md)
RUBY

# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
# Publish the emitted SPINEL tree alongside the run. Campfire has no
# spinel lane — it does not compile yet — so nothing above produces this
# tree and nothing below measures it. It is packed for provenance: a
# dated, stamped record of what we emitted on a given day, so that when
# the tree does become useful to point an upstream issue at, the history
# is already there. It cannot be created retroactively.
#
# Strict on purpose (no --allow-unsupported): campfire's strict emit is
# at 0 errors and CI holds that ceiling, so a regression should be loud
# here rather than silently packed.
#
# The status recorded is what a CONSUMER sees — a plain `spinel -c`, no
# SP_COLLECT_ERRORS. The emit's own error count is the wrong number to
# stamp an archive with: it is 0 today while spinel still refuses the
# tree, so recording it would mark as clean something that does not
# build. Under SP_COLLECT_ERRORS spinel does write C, but the gap sites
# are recovered from, so that output is truncated units rather than a
# program -- also not a status worth recording.
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------
pack_spinel_tree() {
    # Already emitted (and built) above when a spinel lane ran; only
    # re-emit for a run that asked for no spinel lane at all.
    if (( WANT_SPINEL == 0 )); then
        step "transpiling campfire → spinel (archive only)"
        if ! cargo run --quiet --release --bin roundhouse -- --target spinel "$APP" \
                -o "$SP_EMIT" > "$LOG_DIR/emit-spinel.log" 2>&1; then
            fail "strict spinel emit failed (see $LOG_DIR/emit-spinel.log) — tree not packed"
            return 0
        fi
    fi

    local rh sp build_status
    rh="$(cd "$REPO_ROOT" && git rev-parse --short HEAD 2>/dev/null || echo unknown)"
    sp="$(command -v spinel >/dev/null 2>&1 && spinel --version 2>/dev/null || echo 'not on PATH')"
    # The run's own name and where the publisher puts it. A tree in hand
    # has to say which published run it belongs to: the roundhouse SHA
    # dated one (spinel#4503, a box measuring a 09-11 emit on 09-15), but
    # only for someone who could map a SHA to a publish. The stamp and
    # the URL make it one line.
    run="$(basename "$OUT_DIR")"
    archive_url="${CAMPFIRE_ARCHIVE_URL:-https://roundbench.showcase.party/archive/campfire}"

    # One plain front-end run. Non-fatal either way: this records the
    # state, it does not gate on it.
    # Not `-o /dev/null`: spinel refuses to write over a file it did not
    # write, and /dev/null is one, so every archive since that refusal
    # landed said "front end: REFUSED" about a tree that compiled.
    if command -v spinel >/dev/null 2>&1; then
        if (cd "$SP_EMIT" && spinel -c --rbs . main.rb -o "$LOG_DIR/spinel-front-end.c") \
                > "$LOG_DIR/spinel-front-end.log" 2>&1; then
            build_status="front end: OK (emitted C)"
        else
            # spinel prints warnings on the same `spinel: <file>:<line>:`
            # shape as errors, so a prefix match picks up the first
            # warning rather than the refusal. Filter on the marker.
            build_status="front end: REFUSED — $(grep -E '^spinel: ' "$LOG_DIR/spinel-front-end.log" | grep -v ': warning: ' | head -1 | sed 's/^spinel: //' | cut -c1-160)"
        fi
    else
        build_status="front end: not checked (spinel not on PATH)"
    fi

    # UNQUOTED heredoc — it interpolates $rh, $sp and $(date), which is
    # the point. That also makes every BACKTICK a command substitution:
    # prose written as `spinel` RUNS spinel, splices its stdout into the
    # shipped file and leaks its stderr to the operator's console. Three
    # of them shipped in the 2026-09-04 archive, which is how `wrk`'s
    # usage text ended up in the middle of a sentence. Escape every
    # backtick in this block: \` .
    cat > "$SP_EMIT/REPRODUCE.md" <<REPRO
# campfire — spinel tree, as emitted

Emitted by roundhouse \`$rh\` from once-campfire. Strict emit, no
\`--allow-unsupported\`.

    roundhouse : $rh
    spinel     : $sp
    packed     : $(date -u +%Y-%m-%dT%H:%M:%SZ)
    run        : $run
    published  : $archive_url/$run/
    latest     : $archive_url/latest/spinel-tree.tgz
    $build_status

## What this is

The tree the \`spinel\` lanes on the benchmark page were built from. It
compiles, links and serves: the same binary answers campfire's room
pages under \`wrk\` and holds thousands of WebSocket connections under
\`scripts/campfire-cable-sweep\`. Until 2026-09-04 this archive carried a
paragraph saying it did not compile — accurate when it was written, and
long outlived by the tree beside it.

The dated copy is still the point: when a wall here turns out to be
spinel's rather than ours, this is the tree to point the issue at, and it
will not have moved underneath the issue.

## Build it

    # needs spinel + spinel_rbs_extract on PATH
    spinel -c --rbs . main.rb -o out.c    # front end only
    make build                            # through to a binary

Binary media (images, sounds, test fixture files) is excluded — 61 MB of
campfire's emit, none of it read by the compiler. Re-emit from the
roundhouse revision above if you need the assets.

\`SP_COLLECT_ERRORS=1\` on the first command reports every gap in one run
rather than stopping at the first — useful while the count is still
falling, but note it recovers from each gap, so the C it writes is
truncated units and not a program.
REPRO

    # Binary media is 61 MB of campfire's emit and none of it is the
    # subject: a 46 MB test fixture (earth.png), a .mov, the sound
    # library. The spinel compile reads .rb and .rbs only, so the
    # archive carries the source tree and says it left the assets out.
    tar czf "$OUT_DIR/spinel-tree.tgz" -C "$(dirname "$SP_EMIT")" \
        --exclude=build --exclude=bench-work --exclude=storage \
        --exclude='*.sqlite3' --exclude='*.o' --exclude='*.log' \
        --exclude=spin.lock --exclude=node_modules \
        --exclude='*.png' --exclude='*.jpg' --exclude='*.jpeg' \
        --exclude='*.gif' --exclude='*.webp' --exclude='*.ico' \
        --exclude='*.svg' --exclude='*.mp3' --exclude='*.mov' \
        --exclude='*.mp4' --exclude='*.woff' --exclude='*.woff2' \
        "$(basename "$SP_EMIT")" 2>/dev/null \
      && ok "packed spinel tree -> $OUT_DIR/spinel-tree.tgz ($(du -h "$OUT_DIR/spinel-tree.tgz" 2>/dev/null | cut -f1)) [$build_status]" \
      || fail "could not pack the spinel tree"
    rm -f "$SP_EMIT/REPRODUCE.md"
}
pack_spinel_tree

ok "results in $OUT_DIR"
echo
cat "$OUT_DIR/summary.md"
